"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[548] \"That's good,\" Quine approved. \"So, when was the last time you saw Monty Field?\" [549] Morgan cleared his throat uneasily. \"Er... I haven't seen him in a while,\" he whispered in response. \"You see, we used to be partners - successful in legal matters. Then some things happened, and we went our separate ways. Since then, I... I haven't seen him again.\" [550] \"How long ago was that?\" [551] \"About two years and a bit.\" [552] \"Very good.\" Quinn leaned forward. \"I also want to know why you split up.\" [553] Morgan looked down at the carpet, fiddling with his cigar. \"I... uh, I guess you know what kind of person Field is. We had a disagreement about ethics, and after a small argument, we decided to go our separate ways.\" [554] \"Did you part on good terms?\" [555] \"Uh... in that situation, yes.\" [556] Quinn tapped the table. Morgan shifted uncomfortably. He clearly hadn't fully recovered from the shock. [557] \"What time did you arrive at the theater tonight, Morgan?\" the officer asked. [558] Morgan seemed surprised by the question. \"Ah\u2014around 8:15,\" he replied. [559] \"Let me see your ticket stub,\" Quinn said. [560] Morgan fumbled through his pockets before producing it and handing it to Quinn. Quinn took the ticket stub, then pulled out three more from his pockets, placing them under the table and carefully comparing them. He then returned the four ticket stubs to his pocket without expression. [561] \"So, you were sitting in the middle M2 seat, right?\" he said. \"Nice spot, Morgan. What made you want to see 'Gunplay'?\" [562] \"Ah, it's a novelty show, isn't it, officer?\" Morgan looked a bit embarrassed. \"I didn't even know I'd want to come to the theater \u2013 you know, I don't usually go to the theater \u2013 but the manager of the Roman Theater sent me a free ticket for tonight's performance, so I came.\" [563] \"Is that so?\" Quine asked bluntly. \"When did you receive the ticket?\" [564] \"Ah, I got the letter on Saturday morning, officer, delivered to my office.\" [565] \"Oh, you got a letter too, did you? You don't happen to have it on you, do you?\" [566] \"I\u2014very\u2014sure\u2014I brought it,\" he mumbled, starting to rummage through his pockets, \"Here it is! Here you go.\" [567] He handed the officer a small rectangular piece of rough-edged paper made of wrinkled securities printing paper. Officer Quinn carefully took it to the light. Through the printed lines, a clear watermark pattern could be seen. He pursed his lips and carefully placed the paper on the desk's notepad. Under Morgan's gaze, he opened the drawer above Posen's desk and searched until he found a sheet of paper. The paper was large and square, with a luxurious theater seal printed on it. Quinn placed the two papers side by side, pondered for a moment, then sighed, picked up the one Morgan handed him, and read it slowly. [568] The Roman Theater Department cordially invites Benjamin Morgan, Esq. to attend the performance of 'Gunfight' on September 24th (Monday) evening. We sincerely hope that Mr. Morgan, a leading figure in the legal profession in New York, will share his valuable opinions on the social and legal aspects of the play. Of course, we do not impose any obligation. The Roman Theater Department guarantees that Mr. Morgan can accept this invitation without any obligation. [569] (Signature) Roman Theater\n[570] Inviter: S.\n[571] The \"S\" written in ink was sloppy and almost unrecognizable. [572] Quin looked up, smiled. \"Roman Theatre is quite something, Mr. Morgan. I was wondering -\" he continued to smile, gesturing towards Johnson, who had been sitting quietly in the corner, listening to their conversation. [573] \"Call the manager, Johnson,\" Quin said, \"if that publicity man - that Bellson, or Pearson, or whoever - is here, have him come too.\" [574] After Johnson left, Quin turned back to Morgan. [575] \"Could you show me your gloves, Mr. Morgan?\" he whispered. [576] Morgan looked at him perplexed and then threw the gloves on the table in front of him. Quinn picked them up curiously. They were a pair of white silk gloves \u2014 typically worn with evening attire. Quinn pretended to be busy examining the gloves. He turned them over, closely inspecting a smudge on one fingertip, and even tried to put them on his own hand, joking with Morgan. After his examination, he returned the gloves to Morgan with a serious expression. [577] \"Ah \u2014 oh, by the way, Mr. Morgan \u2014 your hat is quite a sight. May I see it?\" [578] Morgan placed the hat on the table, still silent. Quinn picked it up nonchalantly, whistling a tune called \"Broadway,\" a few notes lower than standard pitch. He flipped the hat in his hand. It was a high-quality hat, with a lustrous silk lining and a gold stamp of the manufacturer, \"James Chalmers & Co.\" The initials \"B.M.\" were embroidered on the band. [579] Quinn grinned and put the hat on his own head. It fit perfectly. But he quickly took it off and handed it back to Morgan. [580] \"Thank you very much for allowing me to examine your gloves and hat, Mr. Morgan,\" he said as he pulled out a notepad from his pocket and jotted down a note hastily. [581] The door opened, and Johnson, Pensel, and Harry Nelson entered. Pensel hesitated before approaching, while Nelson sat in an armchair. [582] \"What can I do for you, officer?\" Pensel asked nervously, trying to ignore the dejected, silver-haired figure sitting in the chair. [583] \"Mr. Pensel,\" Quinn said calmly, \"In your Roman Theatre, how many kinds of writing paper do you have?\" [584] The manager's eyes widened. \"Just one, officer. There's one right in front of you on the table.\" [585] \"Ah \u2013\" Quinn handed Pensel the paper he had taken from Morgan. \"I want you to take a good look at this paper, Mr. Pensel. Do you know if the Roman Theatre has such paper?\" [586] The manager gave the paper a strange look. \"No, I don't think we have such paper. In fact, I'm quite sure. What does it say?\" He turned around and called out, \"Nelson! What is this?\u2014Is it your latest promotional gimmick?\" He waved the paper in front of Nelson. [587] Nelson took the paper from the manager and quickly read it. \"Ah, it seems I'll have to change careers,\" he whispered. \"This is bound to break the advertising record.\" He read it again, his face showing envy. Then, under the gaze of four reproachful eyes, he handed the paper back to Pencier. \"I'm sorry, such a brilliant idea didn't even occur to me,\" he said slowly, retreating to the corner and crossing his arms. [588] The manager turned to Quine with a puzzled expression. \"This is strange, officer. As far as I know, the Roman Theater never uses such stationery, and I'm quite certain I never authorized anyone to do such promotional work. If Nelson says he didn't do it\u2014\"\n[589] Quine carefully put the paper in his pocket. \"Alright, thank you, gentlemen,\" he said, nodding at Pencier and Nelson as they left. [590] He looked at Morgan, who was flushed with anger and embarrassment. Quine raised his hand and slammed it lightly on the table again. [591] \"What do you think of this, Mr. Morgan?\" He asked straightforwardly. [592] Morgan jumped up. \"Damn it, this is a frame-up!\" He waved his fist in front of Quinn, \"Please excuse my rudeness, I know no more about this than you do. And don't think you can scare me by searching my hat and gloves - oh, you haven't searched my underwear yet, Inspector!\" [593] \"But my dear Morgan,\" Inspector Quinn said gently, \"why do you have to make yourself so nervous? Others would think I accused you of murdering Monte Field. Sit down, calm down, old boy. I'll ask you a simple question.\" [594] Morgan slumped in the chair, wiped his forehead with a trembling hand, and whispered, \"Sorry, Inspector, I couldn't control my temper. But these unfortunate things -\" He calmed down and murmured. [595] Inspector Quinn sat there, staring at him with a puzzled look. Morgan was busy wiping his sweat with a handkerchief and puffing on a cigar. Johnson coughed disapprovingly and looked up at the ceiling. Suddenly, there was a commotion in the room, but it soon quieted down. [596] Quin's voice broke the silence. \"Well, that's all, Mr. Morgan. You can go now.\" [597] Morgan slowly rose, opened his mouth to say something, but closed it again, put on his hat, and left the room. Johnson saw the officer's gesture and lazily stood up, casually opening the door for him. Both men left. [598] The room was left with Quin alone, who immediately got to work. He pulled out four ticket stubs, the letter Morgan had given him, and the lady's rhinestone handbag he had found in the dead man's pocket. He opened the handbag again, took out its contents, and laid them on the table in front of him. There were several business cards with \"Frances Eves-Popp\" printed elegantly; two delicate handkerchiefs; a small makeup case containing foundation, blush, and lipstick; a small money pouch containing twenty-dollar bills and some coins; and a door key. Quin toyed with these items for a moment, put them back in the handbag, and put the handbag, ticket stubs, and letter back in his pocket. He stood up, slowly scanning the room, then walked over to the coat rack, picked up a top hat hanging there, and checked its lining. It had the initials \"L.P.\" printed on it, and the hat size was 6 3/4, which piqued his interest. [599] He put the hat back in its place, opened the door. [600] The four people sitting in the reception room immediately stood up, their faces expressing relief. Quin stood at the door, smiling, with his hands in his coat pockets. [601] \"It's finally your turn,\" he said. \"Come on, everyone, come into the office.\" [602] He politely stepped aside to let them go first \u2013 three ladies and a young man. They all entered the office with some excitement, the young man hurrying to pull chairs for the ladies to sit down. Four pairs of eyes eagerly looked at the elderly man standing at the door. Quein smiled, like a fatherly figure. He quickly glanced at the reception room, then closed the door, and sternly walked to the table and sat down, reaching for his snuffbox. [603] \"Alright!\" he said mildly. \"I'm very sorry to have kept you waiting so long \u2013 official business, you know. Now, let's get started. Well, right, right, I must \u2013 well, now, first of all, ladies and this gentleman, how shall we begin? I believe, young lady, although I haven't had the pleasure of hearing you introduce yourself, your name should be Frances Eves-Pope, is that right?\" [604] The girl raised her eyebrows. \"Yes, sir,\" her voice was lively and melodious, \"although I'm not sure how you know my name.\" [605] She smiled. Her smile was attractive and seductive, and her gaze was captivating. She was in her prime, graceful and charming, with brown, large eyes and a complexion like jade. She exuded vitality that revitalized Quein's spirits. [606] He smiled at her. \"Well, Miss Eves-Pope, I think for most people, your identity would be a mystery. But I'm a policeman, so I'm stronger than most people, so it's not difficult for me to know your name. And you're definitely not an unknown girl, in fact, I saw your photo in the social section of the newspaper today.\" [607] The girl smiled, a little nervous. \"Oh, so that's how it is!\" She said, \"I'm starting to get scared. Sir, what do you want with me?\" [608] \"Official business - always official business,\" Detective Quin said pitifully, \"I'm interested in anyone, I always relate it to my profession... before asking you questions, I want to ask, who are your friends?\" [609] When Detective Quin looked at the three of them, all three coughed awkwardly. Miss Francis smiled charmingly. \"I apologize, sir, please allow me to introduce my friends. These two ladies are Hilda Orangi and Eve Ellis, my good friends; this is Mr. Stephen Barry, my fianc\u00e9.\" [610] Detective Quin looked at them with a touch of surprise. \"If I'm not mistaken, you're all members of the cast of 'Gunfight', right?\" [611] All three nodded in unison. [612] Queene turned to Francis. \"I don't mean to pry, Miss Eves-Pope, but I want you to explain... why your friends are here with you?\" [613] The three actors stood up very awkwardly. Francis looked from her companions to Queene, pleading with her eyes. [614] \"I\u2014I'm sorry, officer,\" she said quickly. \"I... I've never been questioned by the police before. I'm nervous, and I want my fianc\u00e9 and these two ladies\u2014my closest friends\u2014to be here with me when you ask me questions. I didn't realize it went against your wishes...\u201d\n[615] \"I understand,\" said Queene, smiling. \"I completely understand. But, you see\u2014\" he made a gesture for them to go out first. [616] Stephen Barry leaned over the girl's chair, a challenging look in his eyes as he stared at Quinn. \"Darling, I won't leave if you need me.\" [617] \"But, Stephen, dear\u2014\" Frances helplessly sobbed. Quinn's expression remained firm, showing no signs of relenting. \"You...you should go out first. But please wait outside for me. It won't be long, right, sir?\" she asked, her eyes showing displeasure. [618] Quinn shook his head. \"Not too long.\" His attitude had changed completely, seeming to become somewhat irritable. The other three seemed to sense his change, subtly aligning themselves against him. [619] Hilda Orlandi was a tall, plump woman in her forties, still attractive despite her age, with traces of her youthful beauty on her face. Under the cold light in the room, her face lost its color. She leaned over Frances, staring at Detective Quinn with a firm tone in her voice. \"We'll wait for you outside, dear, if you feel uncomfortable or anything, just call out, and we'll come right in to help you.\" She huffed out of the room, followed by Eve Ellis, who patted Frances' hand comfortingly. \"Don't worry, Frances, we're all here for you.\" She tugged on Barry's arm and followed Hilda Orlandi out. Barry glanced back at Quinn, a mix of anger and concern in his eyes. He glared at Quinn again before slamming the door shut. [620] Quinn immediately stood up, his movements clean and his attitude cold. He stared into Frances' eyes, his hand resting on the surface of the table. \"Now, Frances Eves-Pope, Miss,\" he said succinctly, \"I'd like to talk to you about this...\u201d He reached into his pocket, pulling out a rhinestone purse like a stage magician. \"I'd like to return your purse to you.\" [621] Francis sat up a little, her gaze shifting from Detective Quinn to the sparkling handbag. Her face turned pale. \"Oh, that's... that's my evening bag!\" [622] \"Indeed, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn said, \"it was found at the theater\u2014tonight.\" [623] \"Of course!\" She smiled nervously and sat back down. \"I'm such a fool! I didn't even know my bag was missing until now...\"\n[624] \"But, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Detective Quinn continued solemnly, \"where we found your bag is more important than finding the bag itself.\" [625] Francis opened her mouth wide, staring at him with fear in her eyes. \"Yes, I heard about it.\" She whispered. [626] \"Well, Miss Evans-Pope,\" Quinn coldly continued, \"your bag was found in the pocket of the deceased!\" [627] Fear filled the girl's eyes. She let out a sob and swayed on her chair, her face pale and tense. [628] Quinn leaped forward, a look of pity and concern on his face. He touched Francis's weak body just as the door burst open and Stephen Barry rushed into the room, his coat flapping behind him. Hilda Orlancy, Eve Ellis, and Johnson followed close behind. [629] \"What did you do to her, you damned detective!\" Barry shouted, shoving Quinn aside. He gently held Francis in his arms, brushing aside the strand of black hair that was in front of her face and whispering comforting words in her ear. Francis sighed and looked up at the agitated face next to her, confused. \"Stephen, I...I feel dizzy,\" she murmured, and then sank back into Stephen's embrace. [630] \"Bring some water!\" the young man exclaimed, stroking her hand. Johnson immediately handed over a flat glass cup. Barry forced a few gulps down her throat, and Francis coughed and regained her senses. The two actresses pushed Barry aside and sternly ushered the men out of the room. Quinn followed the protesting young man and the detective out the door with a submissive expression. [631] \"You're a good cop, aren't you?\" Barry sniped at Quinny. \"What did you do to her \u2013 give her a whack on the head with your usual police tactics?\" [632] \"Oh, oh, young man,\" Quinny said mildly, \"don't talk so harshly. She was just frightened.\" [633] They all stood there in silence, the tension palpable. The door opened, and the two actresses emerged, supporting Francis. Barry rushed to her side, stroking her hand and whispering, \"Do you feel better now, dear?\" [634] \"Stephen \u2013 take me \u2013 home,\" she gasped, leaning on Barry. [635] Quinny stepped aside, letting them pass. He watched them slowly make their way to the front door, accompanied by a small crowd of spectators. [636] Chapter Six: The District Attorney Becomes a Biographer\n[637] Detective Richard Quinn was a unique man. Although small in stature, he was remarkably sturdy; his head was full of white hair, and his face was etched with lines that spoke of his rich life experiences.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"I'll keep it to myself, believe me!\" [1861] \"Okay,\" Quin stroked the lining of the hat, which made a clear crackling sound. [1862] \"Now,\" Ellery said calmly, \"we have our first clear indication of why the killer had to take Field's hat on Monday night. The killer's name is likely to be written in the same way - it's an indelible ink, you know - the killer wouldn't leave a hat with his name on it at the crime scene.\" [1863] \"If you find that hat,\" Corning called out, \"you'll know who the killer is!\" [1864] \"Tim, I'm afraid,\" the officer replied calmly, \"that the hat is gone forever.\" [1865] He pointed to the bottom of the inner band connected to the lining. He quickly pulled off those stitches, stuck his fingers into the lining and the hat top, and then silently pulled out a bundle of documents tied together with a thin rubber band. [1866] \"If I were as disliked as some people think,\" Ellery said, leaning back and thinking aloud, \"I would say with complete fairness: 'I told you so.'\" [1867] \"We know when to expect a hit from you, son - don't repeat yourself,\" the officer chuckled, breaking the rubber band and quickly scanning the papers, smiling in satisfaction, and putting them in his jacket pocket. [1868] \"Morgan's, right,\" he said briefly, and began to examine another round-top hat. The inside of the sweatband had a vague X marked on it. The officer found a row of stitches similar to those in the silk hat lining. He pulled out the file - thicker than Morgan's bundle - and curiously checked it. He then handed it to Cronin, who's fingers were trembling. [1869] \"Lucky for you, Tim,\" he said slowly, \"the person you're tracking is dead, but this involves a lot of high-profile people. I think you'll find yourself a hero in a few days.\" [1870] Cronin grabbed the bundle of papers, excitedly spreading them out. \"They're here - they're here!\" he exclaimed, stuffing the stack of papers into his pocket. [1871] \"I have to go, officer,\" he said urgently. \"There's a lot of work to do \u2013 and what you found in the fourth hat is none of my business. I'm grateful to you and Mr. Quinn! Goodbye!\" [1872] He rushed out of the room, and a moment later, the snoring of the policeman in the hallway stopped. The outside door slammed shut. [1873] Ellery and the officer exchanged glances. [1874] \"I don't see how these things are of any use to us,\" the old man muttered, running his fingers over the sweatband of the last top hat. \"We've discovered things and made deductions at a much faster pace than we anticipated \u2013 oh well...\" He sighed, holding the band up to the light. [1875] It read: Miscellaneous. [1876] Chapter 18: The Standoff\n[1877] On Friday afternoon, while Inspector Quin, Ellery, and Tim Cronin were searching the Montague Field house, Officer Wiley, as usual, walked slowly up the brownstone steps of the Quin residence from Broadway to Eighty-seventh Street, rang the bell. Juno answered with a cheerful voice, and the officer came up sternly. [1878] \"The officer's not home!\" Juno announced impulsively, her thin body completely hidden behind the large apron. The smell of onion-fried steak filled the air. [1879] \"Get out of here, kid!\" Wiley shouted. He pulled out a sealed envelope from his chest pocket and handed it to Juno, \"Give it to the officer when he gets back. If you forget, I'll dump you in the East River.\" [1880] \"And who else?\" Juno whispered, his lips trembling, and then he politely added, \"Yes, sir.\" [1881] \"Well, then.\" Wiley turned calmly and went downstairs to the street, his broad back very visible, in stark contrast to Juno's grin in the window on the fourth floor. [1882] Around six o'clock, the two Quinns returned to their residence, and Officer Wiley suddenly noticed the official envelope lying on his plate. [1883] He tore the corner of the envelope and pulled out some documents typed on police stationery. [1884] \"Oh, oh!\" He murmured to the lazily disrobing Ellery. \"The gang is gathering...\"\n[1885] He sat down in the armchair, leaving his hat on and his coat buttoned, and began to read the report. [1886] The first one read:\n[1887] Release Report\n[1888] (September 28, 1920)\n[1889] John Casanelli, also known as Johnny Parsons, alias \"Johnny North Italian,\" also known as Peter Dominic, was granted parole today. [1890] The investigation into John's accomplice in the robbery incident, the Bonomo Silk Robbery (June 2, 1920), has not yielded any further information. We are investigating the informant \"Lovable\" Morehouse for further information, who has disappeared from his usual haunts. [1891] Parole was granted to Jo Casanelli at the suggestion of District Attorney Sampson, but he is under supervision and must be available at a moment's notice. [1892] T. O'W. [1893] The police officer raised his eyebrows and set aside the information about Parson Johnny, picking up the second report which read:\n[1894] Report on William Pusack\n[1895] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1896] An investigation into William Pozsak's history revealed the following:\n[1897] Thirty-two years old; born in Brooklyn, New York, with parents naturalized; unmarried; normal habits; enjoys socializing; has \"dates\" three to four nights a week; religious. [1898] Since Monday evening, his activities have been normal. No letters sent out, no withdrawals from the bank, and his life has been quite normal. No suspicious behavior. [1899] Esther Jablow, a girl who seems to be Pozsak's \"most stable companion,\" has seen him twice since Monday: during lunchtime on Tuesday and Wednesday evening. On Wednesday evening, they went to the movies and a Chinese restaurant. [1900] The fourth informant\n[1901] (T. Weir: Agreed)\n[1902] The officer snorted and tossed the paper aside. The third report is titled:\n[1903] Report on Maggie O'Connell\n[1904] (September 28, 1920)\n[1905] O'Connell lives at 1436 Tenth Avenue. Renting an apartment on the fourth floor. No father. Since Monday evening, she has been idle due to the closure of the Roman Theater. She left the theater with the audience on Monday evening. She went home but stopped at a pharmacy on Eighth Avenue and made a call on Forty-eighth Street. The call could not be traced. Overheard conversation on the phone involved Johnny Parsons. She seemed excited. [1906] She did not leave the house until 1 pm on Tuesday. She did not contact Parson Johnny in Topeka. After discovering that the Roman Theater was indefinitely closed, she went to a nearby employment agency to seek a usher's position. [1907] There were no new developments on Wednesday and Thursday. She returned to work at the Roman Theater on Thursday evening after receiving a call from the manager. She did not meet or contact Parson Johnny. There were no incoming calls, no visitors, and no letters. She seemed suspicious \u2013 she \"knew\" she was being followed. [1908] The Eleventh Detective\n[1909] (Tommy Weir: Agreed)\n[1910] \"Hmm!\" the officer muttered, picking up the next report. \"Let's see what this one says...\"\n[1911] Report on Francis Evans-Pope\n[1912] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1913] After being released from the manager's office at the Roman Theater on Monday evening by Inspector Quin, Francis Evans-Pope immediately left the theater. She was checked along with other audience members at the main entrance. She left with Eve Ellis, Stephen Barry, Hilda Orrance, and other actors. They took a taxi to Evans-Pope's residence on the riverside. She was in a semi-conscious state when she was taken out. The three actors left the house afterward. [1914] She didn't leave the house on Tuesday. The gardener informed her that she spent the whole day in bed. She received many phone calls that day. [1915] She didn't officially appear until Wednesday morning when she met Inspector Quin at her residence. After the meeting, she left the house with Stephen Barry, Eve Ellis, James Pear, and her brother Stanford. Evans-Pope drove them in a luxury car to Westchester. The outing revitalized her. She spent the evening at home with Stephen Barry, playing bridge. [1916] On Thursday, she went shopping on Fifth Avenue and had lunch with Stephen Barry. He took her to Central Park, and they spent the afternoon outdoors. Barry escorted her home before 5 PM. He stayed for dinner, and after dinner, he received a call from the stage manager to return to the Roman Theater. Ives-Pope spent the evening at home with her family. [1917] There was no report on Friday morning. There were no suspicious behaviors throughout the week. She did not speak to any strangers. She did not contact Benjamin Morgan. [1918] The 39th Informant\n[1919] (Agreed by T. Wharton)\n[1920] \"That's all,\" the officer said quietly. He selected the next report, which was quite short. [1921] Report on Oscar Lourne\n[1922] (September 28, 19XX)\n[1923] Lourne worked with Mr. Stoutz and Mr. Croning in his office at Monte Field on Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday mornings. The three had lunch together every day. Lourne is married and resides at 211 Sixteenth Street in the Bronx. He spends his evenings at home. There are no suspicious letters or phone calls. No bad habits. His life is strict and modest. His reputation is good. [1924] 16th Street Informant\n[1925] Note: Detailed information about Oscar Lourne's background and habits can be obtained through Assistant District Attorney Timothy Croning. [1926] (T. W.: Agreed)\n[1927] The policeman sighed and put five files into a plate, stood up, took off his hat and coat, and threw them with a jerk into the hands of the waiting Juno. He then picked up the last report from the envelope - a large sheet of paper held together with a pin, with a small note on it: \"Memo to L. Quill.\" [1928] The note read:\n[1929] This morning Dr. Plaut sent me the attached report to deliver to you. He regrets that he cannot personally report to you, but the Brubling poison case has taken up all his time. [1930] The signature was the familiar and careless initials of a name that Willy knew. [1931] The attachment was a hastily typed brief message, with the heading of the medical office. [1932] Dear Quer: This is about the poison tetraethyl lead. Jones and I have investigated all possible sources of transmission, but without success. I believe you are just going to have to rely on luck in this matter. You will never trace the poison that killed Monte Field. This is not only my humble opinion, but also that of the chief and Jones. We all agree that the most logical explanation is gasoline. Try to find that thing, detective! [1933] Dr. Plauti's postscript read:\n[1934] Of course, I will notify you immediately if anything is found. Stay alert. [1935] \"Useless!\" the policeman muttered, while Ellery silently ate the delicious meal prepared by Zunna. The policeman poked at the fruit salad, looking very unhappy. He grumbled quietly, glanced at the report on the plate with annoyance, stared at Ellery's tired face and his chin devouring the food, and finally threw away the spoon. [1936] \"In all the useless, exasperating, empty reports I've ever seen\u2014\" he roared. [1937] Ellery smiled. \"Of course, do you remember Perianthus? \u2026 Ah? You may be polite, sir \u2026 Perianthus of Collins Bay, who said in his lucid moments, 'Diligence can do anything!'\" [1938] The fire was roaring, and Jones curled up in his favorite corner on the floor. Ellery smoked, comfortably gazing at the flames, while old Quin filled his nose with snuff. The two Quins settled down to a serious discussion. More precisely \u2013 Quin Inspector settled down, talking seriously to his son, as Ellery seemed to be in an extremely dreamy mood, far from the unpleasant details of crime and punishment. [1939] The old man slapped his hand on the armrest of the chair. \"Ellery, have you ever seen such a perplexing case in your life?\" [1940] \"On the contrary,\" Ellery commented, half-closing his eyes and staring at the fire, \"it's quite natural for you to be nervous. You're getting worked up over something as trivial as arresting the killer. Please forgive my hedonistic philosophy \u2026 if you recall, in my story called 'The Black Window Incident,' the detective I wrote about had no difficulty capturing the criminals. Why? Because they stayed calm. Conclusion: always stay calm \u2026 I'm thinking about tomorrow. A pleasant holiday!\" [1941] \"For a gentleman, son,\" the officer barked angrily, \"your lack of coherence is surprising. Your words mean nothing, and your silence speaks volumes. No, I'm confused... \"\n[1942] Ellery burst into laughter. \"Maine's forests - brown - lakeside charming cabins - fishing rods - air - oh, Lord, will tomorrow ever come?\" [1943] The elder Quine looked at his son with a mixture of pity and warmth. \"I... I hope... Hmm, don't mind. I mean, Ellery, if my little thief fails... Then it's all over.\" [1944] \"Damn thief!\" Ellery exclaimed. \"What does Pon have to do with human suffering? My next book is almost finished, dad.\" [1945] \"Stealing inspiration from real life, you scoundrel,\" the old man muttered, \"if you use the case of Field as a plot, I would be very interested in reading the last few chapters you wrote!\" [1946] \"Poor Dad!\" Ellery chuckled, \"Don't take life so seriously. Fail and fail, as they say. Anyway, Monte Field wasn't worth a dime.\" [1947] \"That's not the point,\" the old man said, \"I don't want to admit defeat\u2026 The motives and conspiracies in this case are tangled like a ball of yarn, Ellery. In my entire career, I've never encountered such a tough nut to crack. It's enough to give you a stroke! I know who killed the person\u2014I know why they did it, and I even know how they did it! But where am I?\u201d He paused, took out a pinch of snuff, \"I'm in the middle of nowhere, right there!\u201d He roared, then gradually calmed down. [1948] \"Certainly a very unusual situation,\" Ellery murmured, \"However, the more difficult problem has been solved\u2026 Hey! I can't wait to bathe in the idyllic stream!\" [1949] \"You might get pneumonia,\" the officer said worriedly, \"Now promise me, young man, that you won't do any of those silly things that go against nature while you're there. I don't want to plan a funeral\u2014I\u2026\"\n[1950] Ellery suddenly fell silent, looking at his father. The officer's face seemed unusually aged under the flickering light of the fire, and the pain on his face made his deep wrinkles more human. His hands looked fragile and worried, gently brushing back his thick gray hair. [1951] Ellery stood up, blushing and hesitated for a moment before quickly bending forward and patting his father's shoulder. [1952] \"Cheer up, dad,\" he whispered. \"If it weren't for Sandford and me arranging things... everything would be fine\u2014believe me. If there's any way I can help you, even a little... but there isn't. It's your job now, dad, and no one can handle this problem better than you in the world...\"\n[1953] He disappeared into the bedroom. Juno sat in the corner in a Turkish manner, quickly getting up and walking across the room to the officer's chair. He sat on the floor, leaning his head against the old man's knees. The silence was occasionally broken by the crackling of wood in the fireplace and the dull sound of Ellery moving around in the next room. [1954] Inspector Quin was tired. His face was pale, gaunt, and wrinkled, looking like a relief in the dim red light. He stroked Juno's curly hair. [1955] \"Juno, boy,\" he whispered, \"don't become a policeman when you grow up.\" [1956] Junna turned his head, looking solemnly at the old man. \"I want to be like you,\" he announced. [1957] The phone rang, and the old man jumped up. He grabbed the phone from the table, his face pale and his voice choked. \"This is Quin,\" he said. [1958] After a moment, he put down the phone and staggered towards the bedroom. He leaned heavily against the doorframe. Ellery straightened up from his suitcase - and jumped forward. [1959] \"Dad!\" he cried, \"What's wrong?\" [1960] The inspector tried to smile. \"It's just... a little tired, son, I think,\" he grunted, \"I just got a tip from a small-time crook... \"\n[1961] \"That-\"\n[1962] \"He found nothing.\" [1963] Ellery took his father's arm and led him to a chair by the bedside. The old man slumped into the chair, his eyes tired and weary. \"Ellery, son,\" he said. \"The last piece of evidence is gone. It's enough to drive you crazy! There's no solid evidence in court to prove the killer guilty. What do we have? A series of perfect reasoning - that's all. A good lawyer can find loopholes in the case... Oh! It's not over yet.\" [1964] He got up from the chair, suddenly serious. He patted Ellery's broad back with vigor. \"Go to bed, son,\" he said. \"You have to get up early tomorrow. I'll stay up and think about it.\" [1965] Interlude\n[1966] Please note\n[1967] The current detective novel craze places the reader in the position of the chief detective. I convinced Ellery Queen to allow me to add a challenge to the reader in \"The Roman Hat Mystery\" \u2013 \"Who killed Monte Field?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIn my examination, I found a dinner bag in the back pocket of his suit. Through some business cards and personal documents in the bag, I confirmed that it was yours. I thought to myself, 'Ha! A lady in trouble!' \u2013 it's a natural thing. I sent a man to find you to explain this most suspicious situation. You came \u2013 but when you saw your bag and knew where it was found, you fainted. At that moment, I told myself, 'This young girl knows something!' \u2013 it's a natural conclusion. Now, how can you make me believe that you don't know anything, and that you fainted only because you were frightened by the news? Remember, Miss Francis \u2013 I am not asking this as Richard Quin, but as a policeman who seeks the truth.\" [1262] \"What I say may not be as enlightening as you think, officer,\" Francis replied calmly, breaking the silence after Quin's question ended, \"I don't think it will help you at all. Some facts may not seem important to me, but they may be meaningful to someone who is trained \u2013 more or less, here's what happened. [1263] \"I naturally came to the Roman Theater on Monday evening. Since I've been engaged to Mr. Barry \u2013\" Mrs. Eves-Pope sniffed, and her husband continued to stare at a point above his daughter's head. \"I've gotten into the habit of meeting him after the show, so I often stop by the theater on my way home. At that time, he either takes me home or takes me to some nearby place for dinner. Usually, when we meet at the theater, we make arrangements in advance. Of course, if the timing is right, I sometimes go there suddenly, as I did on Monday night. [1264] \"I arrived at the Roman Theater just a few minutes before the first act ended. I've seen 'The Gunfight' many times. I sat in my usual seat \u2013 Mr. Barry had arranged it through Mr. Pensler weeks ago \u2013 before I could settle down to enjoy the show, the curtain had fallen, and the first intermission had arrived. I felt a bit hot, and the air inside wasn't very good \u2013 so I went to the ladies' room downstairs, then came back up and walked through the open door into the alley. There were already many people there breathing fresh air. [1265] She paused. Ellery leaned against the bookshelf, watching the facial expressions of this small audience with keen eyes. Mrs\n[1266] The officer cleared his throat. [1267] \"Which alley, Miss Francis - the left or the right?\" he asked. [1268] \"The left, officer,\" she answered immediately, \"You know, I sit in the left seat M8, and it's natural for me to come to that side alley.\" [1269] \"Indeed,\" Quinn said with a smile. \"Please continue.\" [1270] \"When I entered the alley,\" she continued, no longer as tense, \"I didn't see anyone I knew, so I leaned against the brick wall of the theater, a little further back from the open iron door. The air after the rain was very refreshing and pleasant. I stood there for less than two minutes when someone gently touched me. I naturally moved a little to the side, thinking that this person had tripped. But when he - it was a man - touched me again, I was frightened and prepared to leave. He... He grabbed my wrist and pulled me back. We were almost hidden by the iron door, which was not fully opened, so I wondered if anyone saw us.\" [1271] \"I see \u2014 I see,\" the policeman said sympathetically in a low voice. \"It seems quite unusual for a stranger to do this in a public place.\" [1272] \"He seemed to want to kiss me, officer,\" said Miss Francis. \"He leaned over and whispered, 'Good night, dear!' \u2014 well, that's just my conjecture. I stepped back a little and said as coldly as I could, 'Please let me go, or I'll scream.' He just smiled and moved closer. The smell of whisky on his breath was overpowering and disgusting.\" [1273] She stopped. Eve Ellis patted her hand to comfort her. Barry rose half up, wanting to protest, and was gently pushed back by Pyle. \"Miss Francis, I'd like to ask you a particular question \u2014 you may find it quite strange,\" said the policeman, leaning back in his chair. \"From the smell of whisky on his breath, can you tell if it was good whisky or cheap whisky? \u2026 You see! I knew you'd laugh.\" Everyone chuckled at the strange expression on Quinn's face. [1274] \"Well, officer \u2014 this question is quite difficult to answer,\" said the girl candidly. \"I'm afraid I'm not very familiar with whisky. But in my memory, that smell should be coming from good whisky. Good whisky \u2014 but too much of it!\" She shook her head sadly. [1275] \"If I were there, I'd be able to tell you what whisky it was right away,\" muttered Stanford Evans-Pope. [1276] His father's lips were tightly closed, but soon relaxed into a smile. He shook his head at Stanford, warning him. [1277] \"Go on, Miss Francis,\" the officer said. [1278] \"I was so scared then,\" the girl admitted, her red lips still trembling, \"and felt sick - I broke free from his hands and stumbled into the theater. I remember sitting down, hearing the warning bell for the second act. I can't remember how I got back to my seat. My heart was in my throat. I remember thinking then that I couldn't tell Stephen - Mr. Barry - about this, worried he'd go after that man. You know, Mr. Barry is quite jealous.\" [1279] \"That's all I remember happening on Monday night, officer,\" she continued, \"I know you'll ask me how my wallet ended up with him. Well - I don't know, officer. I swear on my honor, I don't remember anything about the wallet.\" [1280] Quin shifted in his chair. \"What's this, Miss Francis?\" [1281] \"In fact, I didn't realize I had lost it until you showed it to me in the manager's office,\" she answered bravely, \"I remember taking it with me to the bathroom after the first act ended, and leaving it open, using my powder puff. But whether I left it in the bathroom or dropped it somewhere else later, I don't know.\" [1282] \"Did you ever think, Miss Francis,\" Quinn interrupted her, reaching for his snuff box and then putting it back in his pocket, embarrassed by Mrs. Eves-Pope's cold stare, \"that perhaps the gentleman came to bother you and dropped it in the alley?\" [1283] The girl's face lit up with relief. \"Yes, officer!\" she exclaimed, \"I've always thought so, but this explanation sounds lame - I was too afraid to explain it to you like that! I really can't remember, does this explanation seem logical to you? - when he grabbed my wrist, I dropped my wallet on the ground, and then completely forgot about it.\" [1284] The officer smiled. \"On the contrary, my dear,\" he said, \"this is the only explanation that makes sense of the whole thing. He probably found your wallet there - picked it up - in a half-drunk, half-awake state, out of love, he put it in his pocket, probably intending to return it to you later. That way, he would have had the opportunity to see you again. He seems to have been completely infatuated with you, my dear - and that's no wonder.\" [1285] \"Now - one more thing, Miss Francis, and this investigation will be over,\" Quinn continued, \"can you describe his appearance?\" [1286] \"Oh, of course!\" Miss Francis replied quickly. \"He made a deep impression on me, you can imagine. He's a little taller than me\u2014around five feet eight inches\u2014a bit overweight. His face is puffy, with two deep, pale gray bags under his eyes. I've never seen such a debauched person. He shaved his beard clean. Apart from his prominent nose, there's nothing remarkable about him.\" [1287] \"That should be our friend, Monty Field,\" the officer said firmly. \"Now\u2014think carefully, Miss Francis, have you ever seen him anywhere else\u2014do you know him?\" [1288] The girl answered immediately: \"I don't need to think, sir. I can assure you, I've never seen this man in my life!\" [1289] Ellery's calm and even voice broke the ensuing silence, and everyone turned to look at him in surprise. [1290] \"Miss Francis, forgive me for interrupting your speech,\" he said kindly, \"but I'm curious, did you notice how the man who approached you was dressed?\" [1291] Francis smiled at Ellery, and he winked back at her. \"I didn't pay much attention to his clothes, Mr. Queen,\" she said, flashing her bright white teeth, \"but I seem to remember him wearing a suit - with a stain on his shirtfront - like a wine stain - and a high-crowned hat. In my memory - of course, except for that stain on his shirt - he was very particular about his clothes and had good taste.\" [1292] Ellery quietly thanked her, then leaned back on the bookshelf. Mr. Queen looked sharply at his son and stood up. [1293] \"So, ladies and gentlemen, that's the end of the investigation. I think we can safely say that this matter is settled.\" [1294] There was a round of applause from the crowd, and everyone gathered around Francis, who was glowing with happiness. Barry, Pierce, and Eve Ellis escorted Francis triumphantly out of the room. Stanford looked sad and carefully extended his arm to his mother. [1295] \"The first lesson is over,\" he announced solemnly. \"Mother, hold on to my arm before you faint!\" Mrs. Avery-Pop swayed heavily on her son's arm, protesting as she left the room. [1296] Mr. Eves-Pope shook Queene's hand firmly. \"So, you think this matter is over with my daughter?\" he asked. [1297] \"Yes, Mr. Eves-Pope,\" the officer replied. \"Thank you for your hospitality, sir. We must be going now \u2013 there's much to do. Shall we go together, Henry?\" [1298] Five minutes later, Queene, Ellery, and District Attorney Sampson strode down Riverside Drive side by side, discussing the morning's events as they walked towards 72nd Street. [1299] \"I'm glad that line of inquiry has been cleared up and yielded nothing,\" Sampson mused. \"I must say, I'm impressed by the girl's courage, Que!\" [1300] \"A good kid,\" the officer said. \"What do you think, Ellery?\" he asked suddenly, turning to his son, who was staring straight ahead at the river. [1301] \"Oh, she's quite charming,\" Ellery replied quickly, his dazed eyes suddenly brightening. [1302] \"I'm not talking about the girl, my son,\" the father said impatiently. \"I'm talking about the overall situation this morning.\" [1303] \"Oh, that!\" Ellery smiled slightly. \"You don't mind if I quote an Aesop's fable, do you?\" [1304] \"I do mind,\" the father grumbled. [1305] \"The lion,\" Ellery said, \"may benefit from a mouse.\" [1306] Chapter 13: The Quinns on the Quinns\n[1307] At 6:30 that evening, after dinner, Juno had just cleared the table and was bringing coffee for the Quinns when the doorbell rang. The all-purpose helper straightened his tie, pulled down his jacket, and strode purposefully into the hallway (the police officer and Ellery watched him with interest). [1308] \"So formal, Juno!\" he muttered. \"Oh, oh! Dr. Plauti brings a guest. Let them in, kid!\" [1309] Juno went out again, bringing in the assistant medical examiner and a tall, thin man with a bald head and meticulously groomed beard. The Quinns stood up. [1310] \"I've been waiting to hear from you, doctor!\" Quinn grinned and shook Plauti's hand. \"If I'm not mistaken, this must be Professor Jones! Welcome to my home, doctor.\" The frail man bowed. [1311]\u201cThis is my son, my conscience keeper, doctor.\u201d Quinns added, introducing Ellery. \u201cEllery\u2014this is Dr. Siddles Jones.\u201d\n[1312]Dr. Jones extended his soft, large hand. \u201cSo, you must be the young man that Quinns and Sampson have been talking about!\u201d He spoke in a deep, resonant voice. \u201cIt's a pleasure to meet you, sir.\u201d\n[1313]\u201cI've been looking forward to meeting our city's Paracelsus, the famous toxicologist.\u201d Ellery smiled. \u201cYour name sends shivers down the spines of New Yorkers.\u201d He pretended to shudder and pointed to the chair next to him. The four sat down. [1314]\u201cHave some coffee, gentlemen,\u201d Quinns suggested, then called out to Juno, who was peeking out from the kitchen doorway. \u201cJuno! You little scamp! Bring us four cups of coffee!\u201d Juno grinned and disappeared behind the door. Soon he popped up, like a toy from a toy box, bearing four steaming cups of coffee. [1315]Plautti looked like the devil himself as he pulled a frightening-looking black cigar from his pocket and puffed away vigorously. [1316] \"Idle chatter is only suitable for people like you,\" he said after taking a puff of smoke, \"But me, I spend my days analyzing the contents of a woman's stomach, just so I can go home and get some sleep.\" [1317] \"I know, I know!\" Ellery muttered, \"I learned from seeking your help from Dr. Jones that you encountered difficulties in analyzing Mr. Field's body. Tell me, my Esquilp.\" [1318] \"I will,\" Plautus replied gravely. \"You're right - I encountered a major difficulty. Professionally speaking, I can humbly say that I have some experience in examining the organs of deceased ladies and gentlemen, but I admit I've never seen anyone's organs as messed up as this guy's. I mean it, Jones can vouch for that. For example, his esophagus and entire trachea looked like someone had sprayed them with a blowtorch.\" [1319] \"What - it couldn't be mercuric chloride, could it, doctor?\" Ellery asked. He was proud of his limited knowledge of precise science. [1320] \"No, impossible,\" Plautus said in a low voice. \"Let me tell you what happened. I analyzed all the poisons mentioned in the records, but despite its petroleum-like composition, I still couldn't pinpoint it accurately. Yes, sir - I'm truly at a loss. And let me tell you a secret - the coroner thought I was overworking myself and seeing things, so he tried it himself with that delicate Italian hand of his. The result, my friends, was zero. So, let's leave this problem to our fountain of knowledge and let him tell us his experience.\" [1321] Dr. Sauls Jones cleared his throat ominously. \"Thank you for the dramatic introduction, my friend,\" he said in a low voice. \"Yes, the body was turned over to me. At this point, I must say that this is the most shocking discovery in the fifteen years of my career at the toxicology office.\" [1322] \"Good heavens, heavens!\" Quinlow whispered, taking a pinch of snuff. \"I'm beginning to admire the intelligence of our murderer's friend. Recent events suggest that this murder is anything but ordinary. What did you find, doctor?\" [1323] \"I naturally assumed that Platt and the coroner had done a thorough job preparing,\" Dr. Jones began, crossing his bony legs, \"and they usually do. So, I first analyzed this little-known poison. Little-known, I mean, from the criminal's point of view. To illustrate how thorough my search was\u2014I even considered one of the backup poisons favored by our science fiction novelist friends: arrow poison, a toxin from South America. Five detective novels out of four use this poison. But even the most criticized arrow poison in its family disappointed me...\"\n[1324] Ellery leaned back and laughed. \"If you're being sarcastic about my profession, Dr. Jones, I can tell you that none of my novels have used arrow poison.\" [1325] The toxicologist's eyes lit up. \"So, you're a detective novelist too, eh? Quinlow, old pal, let me express my condolences to you... Nevertheless, gentlemen, when it comes to explaining the rarity of the poison, we can usually draw a clear conclusion\u2014namely, that it is a rare poison in the pharmacopoeia. Of course, we know very little about many rare poisons\u2014especially Eastern drugs.\" [1326] \"Well, to put it briefly, I found myself in a dilemma that made me very unhappy.\" Dr. Jones recalled the situation and chuckled softly, \"That conclusion was indeed unhappy. The poison I analyzed had some familiar properties, like Prouty said, but completely different other properties. I spent most of last night carefully studying my flasks and test tubes, and suddenly found the answer in the middle of the night.\" [1327] Ellery and Quin sat up straight, and Dr. Prouty sighed and relaxed in his chair, reaching for another cup of coffee. The toxicologist uncrossed his legs and spoke in a deep, resonant voice. [1328] \"The poison that killed your victim, officer, is called tetraethyl lead!\" [1329] For a scientist, Dr. Jones' high-pitched announcement would have had a dramatic effect. But for the detective, it meant nothing. For Ellery, he just murmured, \"Sounds like a mythical monster!\" [1330] Dr. Jones smiled slightly and continued, \"Didn't you notice that? Let me tell you about tetraethyl lead. It has almost no smell \u2013 more precisely, its physical properties are like trichloromethane, that's the first point. Second \u2013 it has a taste \u2013 of course, it's weak \u2013 but it can be confirmed that it smells like ether. Third \u2013 it is very toxic and terrifying. It is very toxic \u2013 let me tell you what kind of damage this potent chemical can do to living tissue.\" [1331] By now, the toxicologist had completely captured the attention of the audience. [1332] \"I took a healthy rabbit, the kind used for experiments, and painted undiluted tetraethyl lead on - notice, just a little - in the soft area behind the ear. Remember, this is not an internal injection, but only applied to the skin. It will be absorbed by the skin and then reach the blood. I observed the rabbit for an hour - after that, I didn't need to observe it anymore, it was dead, just like any other dead rabbit.\" [1333] \"I don't see it as very toxic, doctor,\" the officer questioned. [1334] \"Not strong? Well, believe me, it's extraordinary.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"As far as I know, young man, he only has one. How many does a person need?\" [1615] \"You can be sure of that, I think,\" Ellery said. [1616] \"Absolutely, Mr. Quayle,\" she said in a sweet voice. Ellery stared at her as if she were a strange specimen. She pouted and turned away quickly. [1617] \"I'm not very popular here, so I'll be leaving... You're not going to put me in that disgusting jail, are you, officer? Can I go now?\" [1618] The officer bowed. \"Oh, yes\u2014you can go, Mrs. Rathbone, but under certain supervision... but in a few days, we'll need your charming company again, so I hope you'll understand. Can you stay in town?\" [1619] \"Of course, I promise!\" she laughed and strutted out of the room. [1620] Wiley jumped up like a soldier and said, \"Well, I guess that's settled, officer!\" [1621] The officer slumped into the chair, exhausted. \"Thomas, you're like a silly cop in Ellery's novel \u2014 of course, I mean no offense \u2014 suggesting that Morgan was arrested for the murder of Monte Field?\" [1622] \"How \u2014 could there be anything else?\" Wiley seemed bewildered. [1623] \"We'll have to wait a bit, Thomas,\" the old man replied gravely. [1624] Chapter 16: The Quinns Go to the Theater\n[1625] Ellery and his father sat across from each other in the small office. Wiley sat in the chair, frowning in silence for a moment before suddenly seeming to make up his mind to leave the room. [1626] The policeman toyed with the lid of a snuff box, grinning. [1627] \"You're surprised too, Ellery?\" [1628] But Ellery's expression was serious. \"The woman gave me a Woodhouse-style 'creepy feeling,' he said tremblingly. 'Scared' is too mild a word.\" [1629] \"I couldn't quite grasp the meaning of her attitude,\" Detective Quin said. \"I thought she knew, and we were just fumbling around... I was at a loss.\" [1630] \"I think this meeting was quite successful,\" Ellery commented. \"Mostly because I've been collecting interesting facts from this long-winded book about handwriting. But Mrs. Angela Rizzo doesn't fit my idea of the perfect woman...\"\n[1631] \"I'd say,\" chuckled the inspector, \"our fair friend has taken a shine to you. Seize the opportunity, son!\" [1632] Ellery made a face of distaste. [1633] \"All right!\" Inspector Queer picked up the telephone on the desk. \"You think we should give Benjamin Morgan another chance, Ellery?\" [1634] \"He's not worth it,\" Ellery grumbled. \"But it's routine.\" [1635] \"You forgot the documents, son\u2014the documents.\" The inspector's eyes twinkled. [1636] He spoke to the operator at the police station in a cheerful tone, and after a while the phone rang. [1637] \"Good afternoon, Mr. Morgan!\" Quin said jovially. \"How are you today?\" [1638] \"Is this Inspector Quin?\" Morgan hesitated slightly. \"Good afternoon, sir. Has there been any progress in the case?\" [1639] \"That's a good question, Mr. Morgan,\" the policeman laughed. \"But I'm afraid I can't answer that yet, I'm afraid I might be accused of incompetence... Mr. Morgan, are you free tonight?\" [1640] There was a pause on the other end. \"Well\u2014to be exact, no. I'm going home, of course, for dinner, and I believe my wife has arranged for bridge. Anything, Inspector?\" [1641] \"I was hoping to invite you and my son to dinner tonight,\" the officer said regretfully, \"Can you come out for dinner?\" [1642] There was a longer pause. \"If it's absolutely necessary, officer...\"\n[1643] \"It's not absolutely necessary, Mr. Morgan... If you accept the invitation, I would be very grateful.\" [1644] \"Oh.\" Now Morgan's voice was more firm, \"Well, then I'll do as you say, officer. Where shall we meet?\" [1645] \"Great, great!\" Quinn said, \"Six o'clock at Carlos Restaurant, how about that?\" [1646] \"Very well, officer.\" The lawyer replied calmly and hung up the phone. [1647] \"I can't help feeling sorry for the guy,\" the old man murmured. [1648] Ellery muttered incoherently, without sympathy. Angela Lashaw's influence was a thorn in his side. [1649] At exactly six o'clock, Quin and Ellery met Benjamin Morgan in the cozy lobby of Carlos Restaurant. He sat dejectedly in a red leather chair, staring at his hand; his lips drooped with worry, and his legs were subconsciously spread apart due to his gloomy mood. [1650] As the Quin father and son approached, he forced a smile and stood up firmly. This gesture conveyed his determination to the two kind-hearted hosts. The officer was in high spirits, partly because he really liked the plump lawyer and partly because of the official business. Ellery was, as usual, ambiguous in his attitude. [1651] The three men shook hands like old friends. [1652] \"I'm glad you could make it on time, Morgan,\" said the inspector. [1653] A reserved waiter led them to a table in the corner. \"I'm very sorry to pull you away from your meal. Once upon a time\u2014\" he sighed, and everyone sat down. [1654] \"No need to apologize,\" Morgan smiled weakly. \"I suppose you both know that every married man occasionally wishes to attend a bachelor's party\u2014like now. Inspector, do you have something to discuss with me?\" [1655] The old man raised his finger and made a warning gesture. \"Not now, Morgan,\" he said. \"I have an idea: Louis has a talent for cuisine\u2014right, Louis?\" [1656] Dinner was a feast of culinary delights. The officer paid no heed to the subtleties of cooking and handed the menu to his son. Ellery had always been fascinated by food and its preparation. The three enjoyed the meal immensely. Morgan initially tasted the food absentmindedly but grew to appreciate the delicious dishes in front of him, eventually forgetting his worries and engaging in lively conversation with the hosts. [1657] They sipped milk coffee, and Ellery carefully smoked a fine cigar. The officer steered clear of cigars, while Morgan eagerly smoked one. Quin then cut to the chase. [1658] \"Morgan, I don't want to beat around the bush. I'm sure you know why I invited you here tonight. I'll get straight to the point. I want you to explain why you haven't mentioned the events of four days ago, on Sunday, September 23rd.\" [1659] At the officer's words, Morgan's expression grew grave. He placed his cigar in the ashtray and stared at the old man with an indescribable weariness. [1660] \"The inevitable always comes,\" he said. \"I knew you'd eventually find out. I suppose Mrs. Rathbone told you everything in her anger.\" [1661] \"Yes,\" Quin admitted candidly. \"As a man of status, I wouldn't listen to rumors; but as a police officer, it's my duty. Why did you keep it a secret, Morgan?\" [1662] Morgan drew a meaningless pattern on the tablecloth with his spoon. \"Because...uh, because people are idiots unless someone tells them how stupid they are.\" He looked up calmly and said, \"I had hoped and prayed\u2014it's a weakness of human nature, I suppose\u2014that this would be a secret between me and the dead. Discovering that whore hiding in that bedroom\u2014hearing every word I said\u2014made me feel so humiliated.\" [1663] He took a big gulp of water and continued, \"Inspector, I thought I had been set up and couldn't provide any favorable evidence. It was true. I found my most hated enemy murdered at the theater, and it happened just a few steps away from me. I couldn't explain why I was at the scene of the crime unless I told a ridiculous, baseless story; in that moment of agony, I remembered that I had argued with the victim the night before.\" [1664] The inspector remained silent. Ellery leaned back in his chair, frowning at Morgan. Morgan swallowed hard and continued. [1665] \"That's why I didn't say anything. Can you blame someone who, when the law clearly warns him that he is digging his own trap, chooses to remain silent?\" [1666] Quinn paused for a moment before saying, \"Let's not talk about that, Morgan. Why did you go to Field's on Sunday night?\" [1667] \"For a good reason,\" the lawyer said painfully, \"Last Thursday, Field came to my office and told me he needed to make a final business investment and needed to find a way to get fifty thousand dollars immediately. Fifty thousand dollars!\" [1668] \"This is very interesting, Mr. Morgan,\" Ellery said, his eyes flashing. \"Which sentence in the conversation made you suspect he was 'settling old scores'?\" [1669] \"Yes, that's why I said it. He gave the impression that he was short of money and wanted to take a vacation\u2014a trip to Europe for at least three years\u2014he was soliciting funds from 'friends.' I didn't know he was blackmailing on a large scale, but this time\u2014\"\n[1670] Ellery and the inspector exchanged glances. Morgan continued speaking. [1671] \"I told him the truth: I'm not in a good financial situation right now, mainly because of him, and I certainly can't come up with the absurd amount he asked for. He just smiled - insisted on the money. Of course, I was eager to get those documents back...\"\n[1672] \"Did you check the canceled checks to see if those documents were actually lost?\" the officer asked. [1673] \"No, officer,\" Morgan said angrily, \"Actually, he showed me those documents and letters two years ago at the Webster Club - we had an argument. Oh, they're fine. He's a man of his word.\" [1674] \"Go on.\" [1675] \"Last Thursday, he openly threatened me and hung up the phone. In the conversation, I tried to convince him that I would find a way to meet his demands, because I knew that once he realized he had squeezed me dry, he would expose those documents without hesitation...\"\n[1676] \"Did you ask him to show you the documents?\" asked Ellery. [1677] \"I think I did - but he laughed and said he would show me the invoices and letters when he saw the money. He's sly, that con artist - he wouldn't risk me killing him while he was pulling out that damning evidence\u2026 You see, I'm being so honest. I can even admit that the thought of violence occasionally crosses my mind. In that situation, who wouldn't think that? But I never really wanted to kill anyone - there's a good reason for that.\" [1678] \"That doesn't benefit you at all,\" Ellery whispered. \"You still don't know where the documents are!\" [1679] \"That's right,\" Morgan nervously laughed. \"I don't know. Those documents could be exposed at any time - fall into someone else's hands - what good would it do for me if Field died? I might just be trading one bad foreman for another\u2026 On Sunday night, I managed to scrape together money for three days, but to no avail - I decided to come to a final reckoning with him. I went to his apartment, found him in his pajamas, very surprised to see me, but without fear. The living room was a mess - I didn't know Mrs. Rask was hiding in the next room.\" [1680] He lit a cigar with trembling fingers. [1681] \"We argued - to be precise, I argued, and he sneered. He didn't care about any reasons or pleas. He wanted the fifty thousand dollars, or he'd expose the details... and the evidence. After a while, I was furious... I left before I lost control. That's it, officer, I swear it on my honor as a gentleman and an unfortunate victim of fate.\" [1682] He turned his face away. Inspector Quin coughed and threw his cigar into the ashtray. He took a brown snuff box from his pocket, took a pinch, took a deep breath, and leaned back in his chair. Ellery suddenly poured Morgan a glass of water, which he drank in one gulp. [1683] \"Thank you, Morgan,\" Quin said, \"since you've been so candid about the details, would you please tell me honestly if you threatened Field's life during your argument on Sunday night. For fairness' sake, I must tell you that Mrs. Rathbone firmly accused you of killing Field.\" [1684] Morgan turned pale, his eyebrows twitched, and his eyes grew vacant and anxious as he stared pitifully at the officer. [1685] \"She's lying!\" he croaked. Several diners at nearby tables looked around curiously, and Inspector Quin patted Morgan's arm. Morgan bit his lip and whispered. \"I didn't do that, officer. I just told you the truth. I had cruel thoughts of killing Field. It was an impotent, stupid, meaningless idea, and I - I didn't have the guts to kill him. Even when I threatened him in the club, I didn't mean it. Of course, on Sunday night - please believe me, don't believe that shameless, money-hungry whore, officer - you have to believe me!\" [1686] \"I just want you to explain what you said. Because,\" Detective Quin said calmly, \"although it seems strange, I do believe you said what she thought you said.\" [1687] \"What did she say?\" Morgan sweat, his eyes bulging. [1688] \"\u2018Expose those documents if it means destroying me - I guarantee it's your last time blackmailing someone!'\" Detective Quin answered, \"Did you say that, Morgan?\" [1689] The lawyer stared incredulously at the Quin father and son, then burst out laughing. \"Oh my god!\" he gasped, \"That's what she thought was a threat? Officer, I meant that if I couldn't meet his extortionate demands, I would turn myself in to the police and drag him down with me. That's what I meant! She thought I was threatening his life -\"\n[1690] Ellery chuckled, motioned for the waiter to come over, paid the bill, and lit a cigarette, glancing sideways at his father. Detective Quin sympathetically and blankly stared at Morgan. [1691] \"Very well, Mr. Morgan,\" the officer stood up, pushing his chair back, \"That's all we wanted to know.\" He politely turned to the side, allowing the dazed and trembling lawyer to exit first, heading towards the cloakroom. [1692] As the Quinn father and sons strolled down Broadway along Forty-Seventh Street, the sidewalk in front of the Roman Theater was packed with people. The crowd surged, and a barricade was set up to maintain order. The entire narrow passage was at a standstill. The strong lights above the marquee flashed the play's title, \"The Gunfight,\" with smaller lights illuminating the cast list: starring James Parrish, Eve Ellis, and a full cast of stars. Men and women elbowed their way through the throng, police shouted for people to show their tickets to pass the barricade. [1693] The officer flashed his badge, and he and Ellery were pushed and shoved along with the throng of spectators into the theater's small foyer. The manager, Poulson, stood beside the ticket booth with a smile on his Latin American face; he was polite, firm, and authoritative, helping customers move quickly from the ticket booth to the ticket check. The doorman, covered in sweat, stood by, looking bewildered. The cashier worked diligently. Harry Nelson was cornered, engaged in intense conversation with three young men who appeared to be reporters. [1694] Poulson spotted the Quinn father and sons and approached them. Seeing the officer's proud demeanor, he hesitated, nodding knowingly, and returned to the cash window. Ellery stood in line, obtaining two reserved tickets from the ticket booth. They wove through the throng of people, making their way to the front row. [1695] When Ellery handed Maggie O'Connell two tickets clearly marked LL32 Left and LL30 Left, she took a step back in surprise. She awkwardly took the tickets, glancing nervously at the officer, who smiled. She led them down the left aisle to the back row, pointing to the last two seats, and quickly left. They sat down, hanging their hats on the metal racks under the seats, and comfortably leaned back, appearing to be two pleasure-seekers, eagerly anticipating the evening's violent and bloody performance. [1696] The auditorium was packed. Crowds of people lined the aisles, being ushered quickly to their seats by ushers. Many people turned to look at the Quin father and son, who had inadvertently become the center of attention. [1697] \"Damn it!\" the older man complained, \"We should have waited until the curtain went up.\" [1698] \"You're too sensitive to public acclaim, Father,\" Ellery laughed. \"I don't mind being the center of attention.\" He glanced at his watch, and the two exchanged meaningful glances. It was 8:25. They shifted in their seats and settled in. [1699] One by one, the lights went out. The chatter of the audience quieted down. In the darkness, the curtain rose on the stage, revealing a mysterious scene. A gunshot broke the silence; a man let out a gurgling cry, and the audience gasped. \"The Gunfight\" opened in a well-known, exaggerated manner. [1700] Compared to his father's intense concentration, Ellery sat back in his chair, relaxed, and enjoyed the excellent melodrama. James Pierce's deep, resonant voice echoed through the stage, captivating him with the dramatic narrative. Eve Ellis' focus on her role was evident \u2013 she was currently speaking in a low, quivering voice with Stephen Barry. Barry's handsome appearance and pleasing voice prompted the young girl sitting next to the officer to exclaim in admiration. Hilda O'Lanci was squeezed into a corner, dressed in flashy attire, fitting her stage persona. The elderly \"character actor\" aimlessly wandered around the stage. Ellery leaned towards his father. [1701] \"This is a strong cast,\" he whispered. \"Look at that O'Loughlin woman!\" [1702] The performance was full of twists and turns, punctuated by the occasional sound effect, accompanied by the symphony of dialogue and noise. The first act ended. The lights came on, and the detective glanced at his watch: 9:05. [1703] He stood up, and Ellery lazily followed. Marcia O'Connell pretended not to see them, pushing the heavy iron door opposite the aisle. The audience poured out into the dimly lit alley.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThrough your investigation of the night watchman and O'Connell, along with the night watchman's testimony, Jesse Lynch was in the alley, the usher confirmed this fact, and everything else \u2026 ponder on it, the killer was in the theater, without a doubt.\" [1399] \"This puts us in a temporary bind. Right now, all we can do is think about the character of the people we encountered during our investigation,\" the policeman sighed and continued, \"First \u2013 Maggie O'Connell said she didn't see anyone walking in the aisle during the second act, did she tell the truth? And she said she didn't see anyone sitting in seat LL30 all night, but we know someone should have been there from 9:30 to the ten minutes or fifteen minutes before the body was found, so was she lying?\" [1400] \"That's a tricky question, Dad.\" Ellery said solemnly, \"Because if she was lying, we lose a lot of clues. If she was lying then \u2013 God help us \u2013 she could describe or even identify, or possibly even name the killer! However, her nervousness and strange demeanor may be due to the fact that she knows that Johnny was in the theater at the time and that many police officers are eager to catch him.\" [1401] \"It sounds reasonable,\" Quinn mumbled. \"Well, what about Parsons Johnny? How did he get involved in this\u2014or was he not involved at all? We must remember, according to Morgan, that Cazzanelli and Field had a close relationship. Field was his lawyer, and perhaps he bribed Parsons to help with the irregular transactions Croaning has been investigating. If Parsons being at the theater wasn't a coincidence, was it through Field or O'Connell? Is it as they both claimed? I think, my son,\" he tugged at his beard, adding, \"I want to give Parsons Johnny a taste of the whip\u2014it won't hurt his thick skin! And that little proud O'Connell girl\u2014scaring her won't do much harm either...\"\n[1402] He took a deep breath of snuff, followed by a series of sneezes, and Ellery's sympathetic laughter accompanied him. [1403] \"And dear old Benjamin Morgan,\" the officer continued, \"the anonymous letter conveniently provided him with a ticket, the source of which was very mysterious. Is he lying about this?\" [1404] \"And then there's the most interesting lady, Mrs. Angela Roxboro... Ah! These ladies, bless them! They always muddle up a man's logic. What did she say\u2014that she'd be at Field's flat by nine-thirty? That her absence was evidence? Of course, the porter confirmed her story. But it's easy to fix a porter... For Field's business\u2014especially his private business\u2014does she know more than she's letting on? Was she lying when she said Field told her he'd be back at the flat at ten? Remember, we know the meeting at the Roman Theater was at nine-thirty\u2014did he really think he could be back home by ten? By taxi, it would take fifteen or twenty minutes on the road, leaving only ten minutes for the transaction\u2014perhaps, of course. The subway wouldn't be much faster. We must remember, this woman didn't show up at the theater all evening.\" [1405] \"Just this beautiful woman alone will keep you busy,\" Ellery said. \"It's obvious that she's hiding something. Have you noticed her brazen contempt? It's not just bluffing. She knows something, Dad. I'll be watching her closely - she'll slip up eventually.\" [1406] \"Haggstrom will take care of her.\" Quinn said absent-mindedly, \"Now, what about Michaels? He doesn't have an alibi for Monday night. But that doesn't matter. He wasn't at the theater... Did he really go to Field's apartment on Tuesday morning to look for something? We've thoroughly searched Field's apartment\u2014did we miss something? He lied about the check thing, and obviously didn't know Field was dead. He must have realized he was taking a risk coming to Field's apartment. He read the paper, he wouldn't want the police there. So, what's his reason for taking the risk?\" [1407] \"Maybe something related to his prison term\u2014I mentioned that, and he looked surprised, right?\" Ellery chuckled. [1408] \"Maybe.\" The detective replied, \"By the way, I found out from Weil about Michaels' stint in Elmira. Thomas reported it was a hush-hush case\u2014much heavier than the light sentence he got in the reformatory. Michaels had been suspected of forgery\u2014it looked serious for him. Then his lawyer, Field, cleverly defended him, and he was charged with a completely different offense\u2014something like a minor theft\u2014and that was the end of the forgery charges. This Michaels seems to have some pull\u2014we have to keep a close eye on him.\" [1409] \"I have a little theory about Michaels,\" Ellery said thoughtfully, \"but let's put that aside for now.\" [1410] Quinn seemed not to have heard Ellery. He stared into the crackling fire in the hearth. \"And Lune,\" he said, \"a man like Lune, so closely connected to his employer, claiming he knows nothing else besides what his employer mentions, seems too hard to believe. Is he hiding something? If so, he'd better pray\u2014because Croaning will soon make him reveal the truth.\" [1411] \"I like Croning,\" Ellery said, \"How does a man muster the determination to do something like that... Have you thought of a question? I want to know if Morgan knew Angela Roxo. Despite both denying it, wouldn't it be interesting if they did?\" [1412] \"Son,\" Quinn complained, \"stop worrying yourself. We have enough on our plate already, so... Ah!\" [1413] The room was quiet and comfortable. Quinn lay down, the flickering firelight illuminating him. Ellery munched on a pastry. Juno sat in a corner, eyes shining with excitement, her small body hunched as she listened to their conversation. [1414] Suddenly, in a moment of mental shift, the old man's gaze met Ellery's. [1415] \"Hats...,\" Quinn murmured, \"We always come back to the hat.\" [1416] Ellery's gaze was puzzled. \"Back to the hat is fine, Dad. Hat - hat - hat! What does the hat have to do with the case? How much do we know about the hat?\" [1417] The officer shifted in his chair. He propped his feet up, took a pinch of snuff, and continued the discussion with renewed vigor. \"Well. We can't be lazy about that damned silk hat. So far, what do we know? First, the hat is still in the theater. Isn't that interesting? After such a thorough search, we couldn't find any trace, which seems impossible... Everyone left, and there was nothing in the cloakroom; there was nothing found in the debris, nothing to suggest that the hat was torn or burned; in fact, there was no clue, no evidence to help us continue the investigation. Therefore, Ellery, in this regard, the only reasonable judgment is that we are looking in the wrong place! Further, wherever it is, it must still be in the theater, because we have been closed since Monday evening. Ellery, we will go back to the theater tomorrow and turn it upside down. Unless we see some light, I won't sleep.\" [1418] Ellery was silent for a moment. \"I'm not satisfied with what you said, Dad,\" he said finally, \"Hat - hat - something is wrong!\" [1419] The old man nodded vigorously. \"Since yesterday morning, when I had time to think about the hat, I felt that we were stuck somewhere. It's already Wednesday evening, and there's no progress. We've done all the necessary work, but we've got nothing... I stared into the fire in the fireplace, \"Everything is a mess. I know all the ins and outs, but for some reason, I can't seem to put them together - explain everything... Son, the missing piece is the key to the whole case.\" [1420] The phone rang. The officer jumped up to answer the phone. He listened carefully to a man's slow speech, commented lightly, and then hung up the phone. [1421] \"It's deep in the middle of the night, who is it? And you talk so much. Oh, you know a lot of secrets, right?\" Ellery grinned. [1422] \"It's Edmund Krueger.\" Quin said, \"You remember, I told him to go check the Roman Theater yesterday morning. He has been there for two days, yesterday and today. And he clearly reported that there was no secret hiding place in the theater. Edmund Krueger has the final say on such architectural issues, and if he says there's no hiding place, you can be sure there won't be any.\" [1423] He stood up and saw Junna squatting in the corner. \"Junna! Make the bed.\" He yelled. Junna quietly walked through the room and left with a silent smile. Quin turned to Ellery, who had taken off his coat and was fumbling with his tie. [1424] \"The first thing we have to do tomorrow morning is go to the Roman Theater and search again.\" The old man spoke firmly, \"Tell you, son \u2013 I hate wasting time! Some people had better watch out!\" [1425] Ellery wrapped his father's shoulders with his muscular arm affectionately. \"Go to sleep, you old cheat!\" He laughed. [1426] This book is compiled by \"ePUBw.COM\". \"ePUBw.COM\" provides the latest and most complete e-book downloads! [1427] Part Three\n[1428] \"Great detectives are born, not made. Like all geniuses, they do not come from the meticulously cultivated police, but from the whole of humanity. The most amazing detective I have ever known is an old, dirty shaman who has never left the jungle... This truly outstanding detective has a unique gift \u2014 extraordinary observation of events, understanding of human thinking, and insight into the human mind. And this gift is the factor that solves the problem, or the unshakable law.\" [1429] \u2014 Excerpt from \"The Hunter's Guide\" by James Redix, Jr.\n[1430] Chapter 14: The Hat Problem Grows Larger\n[1431] On Thursday, September 27, the day after the murder at the Roman Theater, Inspector Queequeg and Ellery got up early and hastily dressed. Juno was dragged out of bed and dressed in plain clothes, looking like a maid in the Queequeg household. They gobbled down a hurried breakfast in Juno's disapproving gaze. [1432] They chewed their tasteless pancakes loudly, and the old man asked Juno to put through a call to Louis Pensel. After a while, the inspector spoke kindly into the phone: \"Good morning, Pensel. I apologize for waking you up so early... Something important has happened, and we need your help. Can you come to the Roman Theater to open it for us? I told you that the theater would not be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1433] Pensel mumbled a comforting phrase sleepily. [1434] \"Can you come to the Roman Theater right away to open it for us?\" the old man continued, \"I told you that the theater wouldn't be closed for long, and now it seems that you can still take advantage of the public's attention to this incident. You know, I can't determine when we'll be able to reopen, but there's a glimmer of hope that it could be tonight. Can you lend me a hand?\" [1435] \"Great!\" Pensel's voice trembled with excitement over the phone line. \"You want me to come to the theater right away? I'll be there in half an hour \u2013 I'm not even dressed yet.\" [1436] \"Alright,\" Quin replied. \"Of course, Pencier \u2013 don't let anyone in yet. Wait for us on the sidewalk before you unlock the door, and don't tell anyone. We'll discuss it further inside the theater... Wait a moment.\" [1437] He held the phone to his chest and looked at Ellery with a questioning gaze. Ellery gestured vigorously, and Quin mouthed a name, nodding in agreement before returning to the phone. [1438] \"Now there's one more thing I need your help with, Pencier,\" he continued. \"Can you find Mrs. Philip, the enthusiastic lady? I'd like her to meet us at the theater as soon as possible.\" [1439] \"Certainly, Inspector,\" Pencier said. The inspector hung up the phone. [1440] \"Well, that's settled,\" he said, rubbing his hands together and reaching into his pocket for a snuff box. \"Ah \u2013 ah \u2013 sneeze! Thank you, Sir Walter and all the hardworking pioneers who defended the tobacco industry!\" He sneezed happily. \"We'll be there in a moment, Ellery.\" [1441] He picked up the phone again and issued a few cheerful orders to the detective headquarters before slamming the phone back onto the table and urging Ellery to put on his coat. Juno looked mournfully at them as they left. He often begged the officer to let him accompany them on their occasional trips to the secluded paths of New York, but the officer had his own ideas about raising teenagers and always refused. Juno regarded his benefactor as if he were a totemic object from the Stone Age, so he had to accept this inevitable outcome and hope for better luck in the future. [1442] It was a cold, damp day. Ellery and his father pulled up their coat collars and walked towards Broadway and the subway. Both were unusually quiet, but their faces expressed a sense of eager anticipation - an uncanny similarity, yet so different - that promised to be an exciting and meaningful day. [1443] They walked briskly along 47th Street towards the Roman Theater. The morning wind was biting, and the streets of Broadway and its spiderweb-like alleys were deserted. A man in a brown coat loitered in front of the closed glass doors of the lobby; another leaned comfortably against the high iron grille separating the left alley from the street. In front of the theater's main entrance, they could see the short, stout Louis Pincus standing and talking to Flint. [1444] Pincus shook hands enthusiastically. \"Well, the ban is finally lifted... it's so nice to hear the news, officer,\" he said. [1445] Pincus fished out a large key ring and unlocked the lobby door. The four of them filed in. The dark-skinned manager fumbled with the lock on the inner door and then pushed it open. The darkened main hall lay before them. [1446] Ellery shuddered. \"Perhaps only the Metropolitan Opera House and Tess's grave are more lifeless than this theatre. It's the perfect place for a deceased relative.\" [1447] The officer wasn't as verbose. He grumbled and pushed his son into the darkened auditorium. \"Cut it out! You're making us nervous.\" [1448] Pincus quickly stepped forward and turned on the main power switch. Under the glare of the giant arc lights and chandeliers, the auditorium took on its familiar contours. Ellery's fanciful metaphor was not as bizarre as his father had suggested. Filthy waterproof canvas covered the rows of long seats; shadows fell on the already dusty carpet; the white plaster wall behind the empty stage was bare, forming an ugly blotch amidst the red plush. [1449] \"I apologize for the waterproof canvas,\" the officer murmured to Pincus, \"because we have to roll them up. We're going to conduct a small-scale search of the auditorium front. Flint, please bring in the two men outside. They might as well make some extra money.\" [1450] Flint hurried out and returned with the two detectives waiting outside the theatre. Under the officer's instructions, they began to pull the large coverings off the rubber chairs, revealing rows of padded seats. Ellery stood near the left aisle, pulling out a small notebook from his pocket, which he had used to sketch the theatre layout on Monday night. He pondered silently, biting his lower lip. He occasionally looked up, as if to verify the layout of the theatre. [1451] Quin quickly returned to the back of the theater, where Poulson was pacing nervously. \"Poulson, we're going to be here for hours. I didn't bring enough people. Can you help me... I need to think about something... just take up a little of your time, but it'll be very helpful to me.\" [1452] \"Of course, Officer!\" the short manager replied. \"I'll be happy to help.\" [1453] The officer coughed. \"Don't think I'm treating you like a runner or anything, old pal,\" he explained apologetically. \"But I need someone who's trained in searching for things like this... and I need some important information from a few people in the district attorney's office who are investigating other aspects of the case. Can you help me deliver a note to a man named Cronin... and bring back a package he gives you? I'm sorry to ask you to do this, Poulson, but it's too important for me to trust it to just any messenger, and\u2014I'm in a bind.\" [1454] Poulson smiled brightly. \"Don't worry about it, Officer. If you need a note, I have paper and pen in my office.\" [1455] The two returned to Poulson's office. Five minutes later, they returned to the auditorium. Poulson hurried out onto the street, holding a sealed letter. Quin watched him leave, sighed, and turned to Ellery. Ellery sat on the armrest of the chair where Field had been killed, still contemplating the pencil sketches he had made. [1456] The officer whispered something to his son. Ellery smiled and patted the older man on the back. [1457] \"Son, shall we take action?\" Quick said. \"I forgot to ask Pencier if he notified Mrs. Philip. I guess he did, or he would have said so. Where is she anyway?\" [1458] Flint was busy removing the waterproof canvas cover with two other detectives, and Quick motioned for him to come over. [1459] \"I told you to do some stretching this morning, Flint. Go up and get busy.\" [1460] \"What am I looking for today, Officer?\" the burly detective grinned. \"Because I hope my luck is better than Monday night.\" [1461] \"You're looking for a hat - a fancy, shiny hat, the kind a stylish person wears, son,\" the officer said solemnly. \"But if you see anything else, call me out loud!\" [1462] Ellery reluctantly took off his coat and slipped the pamphlet into his pocket. The officer twisted and turned to remove his overcoat, leading his son into the corridor. They began to search the audience together, slowly and methodically.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] The first part\n[2] Police must follow the albatross's method of operation - knowing that the hands and clubs of the hobos will bring them great misfortune, they still lay their eggs on the beach... police are no exception. All the process of incubation should not be disturbed by any Japanese. [3] Extracted from Yu Chuan's \"Chien Yeh Collection\"\n[4] Chapter 1: Theater Audience and Corpses\n[5] The opening of the 1920s drama festival was embarrassing. Eugene O'Neill forgot to create a new play in time, so he couldn't get financial support from the \"cultured\" people. As for the \"common people\", after watching a few passionless plays, they gave up the formal theater and went to the movie house to find simpler pleasure. [6] On Monday evening, September 24th, a light drizzle covered the brightly lit Broadway theater district from 37th Street to Columbus Circle. Desolate theater managers and directors could be seen everywhere. Several plays were canceled on the spot by higher-ups, hoping that God and the weather bureau would witness their plight. The rain kept people who should have been at the theater at home by their radios and card tables. Only a few brave souls ventured out into the deserted streets in the rain. Broadway was indeed in a gloomy mood. [7] However, on the sidewalk in front of the Roman Theater on 47th Street, west of \"White Street\", there was a crowd of spectators who only came out during the mid-period of the theater festival when the weather was fine. The signboard of \"The Gunfight\" shone brilliantly under the decorative awning of the theater entrance. The cashier skillfully served the crowd of people waiting in front of the \"Today's Performance\" window. The doorman wore a sober uniform of yellow and blue, his demeanor refined by the passage of time, and was therefore very conspicuous. He bowed to lead the evening's audience into the front row of the auditorium. These spectators wore high hats and leather coats, with satisfied expressions on their faces, as if nothing could stop them from watching \"The Gunfight\" performance. [8] In the latest theater on Broadway, people were hurriedly looking for their seats, obviously a little anxious, as the play's noisy characteristics were well known. At this point, the last chattering audience stopped, the last audience to arrive was tripped by his neighbor, and the lights gradually dimmed, and the curtain slowly rose. A gunshot echoed, a man screamed... the show began. [9] \"The Gunfight\" is the first play of the drama festival to use the characteristic sound effects of the mob. Automatic pistols, machine guns, attacks on nightclubs, mob killings that kill enemies with loud noises - all the clich\u00e9d ways of romanticizing the mob society were woven into the fast-paced three-act play. It exaggeratedly reflected the era - a little vulgar, a little lowbrow, but all in all, it satisfied the audience who watched the play. Therefore, whether it was sunny or rainy, the play was always full and sold out. The situation in the theater tonight was enough to prove its popularity. [10] The performance progressed smoothly. The first act reached its climax with an explosion, and the audience shuddered. The rain had stopped, and people used the ten-minute break after the first act to step out of the theater and breathe in the fresh air after the rain. Then, the curtain slowly rose, and the second act began. The explosions on the stage were even louder. With the sharp-tongued dialogue behind the stage lights, the second act quickly entered the crucial moment. The slight commotion at the back of the theater did not attract attention, as it was normal in the noise and darkness. It seemed that no one noticed anything was wrong, and the plot continued to unfold with a bang. However, the noise of the disturbance gradually increased. At this point, several audience members in the back row on the left began to stir in their seats, angrily defending their rights. This protest was highly contagious, and soon dozens of eyes turned to the front row of the auditorium. [11] Suddenly, a piercing scream echoed through the theater. The audience, engrossed in the rapid events unfolding on stage, craned their necks expectantly in the direction of the scream, hoping for a new dramatic twist in the plot. [12] The theater lights suddenly snapped on, illuminating bewildered, terrified, and enraptured faces, all satisfied with the plot. Near the leftmost closed exit, a tall policeman stood, grabbing the arm of a slightly tense-looking man. The policeman pushed away a crowd of inquisitive people with a loud voice, shouting, \"Stay in your seats and don't move!\" [13] The audience burst into laughter. [14] But the smiles quickly faded. As the audience began to realize that even the actors looked confused and hesitant, despite continuing to recite their lines behind the stage lights, occasionally casting puzzled glances at the front row of the auditorium. People stood up from their seats, uneasy in the face of the anticipated tragedy. The officer's authoritative voice continued to roar, \"I said, sit still! Stay where you are!\" [15] The audience suddenly realized that this was not a play but reality. Women screamed, desperately clutching their companions. The upper-level audience was in a panic, despite not being able to see what was happening downstairs. [16] The officer turned sharply to a small, dark-skinned man dressed in evening attire, standing nearby and fidgeting with his hands. [17] \"Mr. Pensler, I need you to close every exit immediately and make sure they are all locked,\" he barked. \"Assign a usher to each entrance to prevent anyone from entering or leaving. Have someone guard the alley until the police arrive. Move quickly, Mr. Pensler, or there will be trouble.\" [18] Some excited audience members disregarded the officer's loud warnings and crowded around to ask questions. The small, dark-skinned man pushed them away and hurried off. [19] The officer spread his legs and stood at the last exit on the left, using his massive frame to block a person lying on the floor between two rows of seats. The person was dressed in evening attire and lay in an odd position. The officer looked up, grabbed the trembling man beside him, and quickly scanned the back of the auditorium. [20] \"Hey, Nelson!\" he called. [21] A tall, blond-haired man quickly emerged from the small room next to the main exit, pushing through the crowd and approaching the policeman. He looked sharply at the motionless body on the floor. [22] \"What happened, Dohr?\" he asked. [23] \"You'd better ask this guy,\" the policeman said sternly, shaking the arm he was holding. \"Someone died, and this one\u2014\" he glared fiercely at the huddled little man, \"Bosack, William Bosack. Mr. Bosack here says,\u201d Dohr stammered, \"Mr. Bosack says he heard the dead man whisper that he was murdered.\" [24] Nelson stared at the body, dumbfounded. [25] The policeman bit his lip. \"This is a real pain in the ass, Harry,\" he croaked. \"I'm the only cop here, and I have to deal with a bunch of hysterical idiots... I need your help.\" [26] \"Sure, whatever you say\u2014this is quite a mystery.\" [27] Doris suddenly turned around and shouted at the man sitting three rows ahead who was staring at him, \"Hey, get down here! And you guys, back to your seats! Now! Anyone who meddles gets a punch from me!\" [28] He turned to Nelson. \"Harry, get back to your office and call headquarters. Tell them there's a murder here. Have them send a squad over\u2014bring a lot of them. Tell them it's at the theater\u2014they'll know what to do. And, Harry\u2014take my whistle and blow it outside. I need help.\" [29] Nelson walked back through the crowd, and Doris called after him, \"Harry, make sure they send old Quin over.\" [30] The man with light brown hair disappeared from view, returning to his office. Soon, a sharp whistle echoed through the sidewalk in front of the theater. [31] The dark-skinned theater manager followed Doral's orders to have people guard the exits and alleys, and then he rushed back through the crowd. His evening dress shirt was wrinkled, and he wiped his forehead, looking flustered. As he turned to go forward, a woman stopped him. [32] She said sternly, \"Mr. Pensler, what gives this policeman the right to keep us here? I tell you, I have the right to leave! I don't care what's going on here - it's none of my business - it's your business - tell him not to do this stupid thing of locking up innocent people!\" [33] The little man tried to escape while stammering, \"Oh, ma'am, please listen to me. I'm sure this officer knows what he's doing. Someone was killed here - it's serious. You don't see... I'm the theater manager, and I have to follow his orders... please calm down... be patient...\"\n[34] He slipped away from her before she could object. [35] Doral waved his arms frantically and shouted from his seat, \"Everyone, sit down and be quiet! I don't care if you're the mayor, you - the one with glasses - sit down, or I'll take action! Don't you know what's going on? Be quiet, I said!\u201d He jumped down from the seat, wiping the sweat from the brim of his hat and muttering to himself. [36] The main floor was filled with agitation and panic, like a boiling pot. People on the upper levels stretched their necks over the railings, trying to figure out the cause of the commotion below, but to no avail. The audience had forgotten that the performance on stage had been interrupted. Actors mumbled lines from the script by the stage footlights, but the words had lost their meaning. The curtain slowly fell, and the evening's performance ended. The actors chattered nervously as they hurried down the steps. They, like the audience, stared in confusion at the scene of the incident. [37] Among these actors, the plump and radiant elderly lady named Hilda Orlancy, an excellent guest actress, was portrayed as \"Barmaid Murphy\" on the poster; the delicate and elegant \"street urchin Nettie\" was played by Eve Ellis, the female lead; James Peir, the sturdy and tall male lead in \"The Gunfight,\" wore a tweed suit and a hat; Stephen Barry, the young man, looked smart in his evening wear, playing the role of an upper-class youth falling into the \"gangster\" trap. Lucy Hallton's portrayal of the \"streetwalker\" attracted harsh criticism from drama critics during this inappropriate drama festival, as they had little else to criticize or accuse. The elderly man with a pointed beard wore a flawless evening gown, proving that M. Ler Blond, the costume supplier for \"The Gunfight,\" was a remarkable tailor. As the bulky villain looked down at the chaotic audience, his anger on stage disappeared, and a rare gentleness appeared on his face. In fact, all the theatrical personnel, wearing wigs and makeup, rushed down the stage steps, running slowly under the falling curtain, rushing down to the front row of the main floor, and pushing their way through the aisles towards the scene of the disturbance. [38] At the main entrance of the theatre, another commotion made people disregard Dahl's order to stay put and stand up to see what was going on. A group of people in blue uniforms pushed their way in, holding batons. Dahl sighed deeply and saluted the tall man in plainclothes who led them. [39] \"What's going on, Dahl?\" the newcomer asked, frowning at the chaos around him. The blue-uniformed men pushed the audience back to the seating area in the front row. Standing people tried to sneak back to their seats, but were caught and then pushed to the back row, joining the angry crowd. [40] \"Report, Chief, this person seems to have been murdered,\" Dahl said. [41] \"Ah, ha,\" the plain-clothes man glanced indifferently at the motionless body lying at their feet, its face covered by a black-sleeved arm, legs awkwardly spread out, extending under the front row chairs. [42] \"What happened - a shooting?\" the newcomer asked Doyle, turning his eyes. [43] \"No, sir - it doesn't seem to be,\u201d the officer replied. \"I immediately had a doctor from the audience come and check - he thinks it's poisoning.\" [44] The police chief grumbled, \"Who is this?\" He pointed to Poussac, shivering beside Doyle, and suddenly shouted loudly. [45] \"The one who found the body,\" Doyle answered. \"He hasn't left the scene.\" [46] \"Good,\" the detective turned to the crowd of people crammed a few feet behind him and asked, \"Who is the manager here?\" [47] Poulson stepped forward. [48] \"I'm Wiley, the chief of detectives from headquarters,\" the plainclothes man growled. \"Didn't you make these idiots quiet?\" [49] \"I did my best, Chief,\" the manager rubbed his hands and mumbled, \"but they seem to be angry at the policeman's actions,\" he pointed apologetically at Doyle, \"he shouted at them. I don't know how to get them to sit down as if nothing had happened.\" [50] \"Oh, we'll handle it,\" Wiley interrupted him and quickly gave orders to the uniformed men by his side. [51] \"Hey \u2013\" he turned to Dorr and called, \"how about the doors and exits? Have you taken any measures?\" [52] \"Of course, Inspector,\" the policeman grinned, \"I've had Mr. Panzer assign ushers to guard every entrance. They've been there all night. But I've also specifically instructed them to ensure that everything is secure.\" [53] \"Good. Has anyone tried to leave?\" [54] \"I can assure you, Inspector,\" Panzer interjected, \"that we need ushers almost at every exit for this play. It's a crime drama with lots of gunshots, screams, and so on. Having someone at every door enhances the mystery. I can easily check for you if \u2013\"\n[55] \"We'll handle it ourselves.\" Wheeler said, \"Dorr, have you asked anyone to come over?\" [56] \"I called Inspector Quinn,\" Dorr replied, \"I had this publicist Nelson call headquarters.\" [57] Wiley's cold face cracked a slight smile. \"You're quite thorough. And what about this body? Has anyone touched it since this guy found it?\" [58] The man, tightly held by Dorr and huddled up, cried, \"I \u2014 I just found him, sir \u2014 I swear to God, I \u2014\"\n[59] \"Alright, alright,\" Wiley said coldly, \"Are you going to cry all night? What's there to cry about? Hmm, Dorr?\" [60] \"No one has touched it since I arrived,\" Dorr answered, a hint of pride in his voice, \"Of course, except for Dr. Stutgart. I had him called from the audience to confirm that the man was dead. No one else has come close.\" [61] \"You've been busy, Doyle?\" Wiley said. He whirled around to face Pensler, who stepped back. \"Mr. Manager, you'd better go up on stage and announce that everyone stays put until Officer Quinn says they can leave\u2014understand? Tell them to cooperate, the more they cooperate, the sooner they'll be out of here. And make it clear that they stay in their seats, anyone who causes trouble is a suspect.\" [62] \"Yes, yes. My God, what a mess!\" Pensler grumbled as he made his way down the aisle towards the stage. [63] Just then, a small group of people pushed through the stage door at the back of the theater and stepped onto the carpet. [64] Chapter 2: One Quinn Works, Another Quinn Observes\n[65] Detective Richard Quinn was an unremarkable man in both form and demeanor. The elderly gentleman was short and gaunt, with a kindly, placid face. He walked with a slight stoop but moved with a measured pace; he had a full head of white hair and a thick beard, a pair of gray eyes that sparkled with intelligence, and long, slender hands that seemed to complement his demeanor perfectly. [66] As Quine walked quickly across the carpet, eyes from all directions fell upon him, but he left no lasting impression. However, his refined and imposing aura was unique, and his wrinkled face broke into a warm smile, causing a murmur among the audience, which blended perfectly with the rhythm of his progress. [67] The police's changes were more obvious. Doyle retreated to the corner of the left exit. Wiley, the Chief Inspector, stood calmly next to the corpse - while everyone else was almost hysterical, he remained mocking and indifferent. He seemed to willingly give up his central position. The police guarding the aisle saluted the inspector. The tense, grumbling, and angry audience inexplicably relaxed and sat back heavily. [68] Inspector Quine walked up to Wiley and shook his hand. [69] \"It's really a pity, Thomas. I heard about it when you were about to go home.\" He whispered. He smiled at Doyle like a loving father and then glanced at the man on the ground with a hint of pity. \"Thomas,\" he asked, \"are all the exits guarded?\" Wiley nodded. [70] The old man turned around and scanned the surroundings of the scene with interest. He asked Wiley a question quietly, who nodded in agreement; then he beckoned Doyle over with his finger. [71] \"Doyle, where are the people sitting in these positions?\" He pointed to the three chairs next to the corpse and the four chairs in the front row. [72] Doyle looked puzzled. \"I don't see anyone, Inspector... ...\"\n[73] Quinn stood silently for a moment, gesturing for Doyle to return, and then whispered to Wiley, \"It's a crowded room... Do you remember the case?\" Wiley frowned solemnly. \"I feel that this case is a bit tricky,\" the inspector continued in a gentle tone, \"What I can see now is a dead man and a group of sweaty people making a commotion. Let Hessey and Piggott direct the crowd, hmm, child?\" [74] Wiley shouted for the two plainclothes officers who had entered the theater with him. The two men turned around, struggling to squeeze their way to the back, pushing people aside. The police joined the two detectives in their action. The actors were ordered to retreat. The police cordoned off the middle row of seats with a rope, and about fifty people squeezed into the narrow space. The police quietly surrounded them, instructing them to show their tickets and then returning to their seats one by one. Within five minutes, all the audience had returned to their seats. The actors were told to stay within the rope for the time being.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Michaels was Field's so-called personal servant - he mainly acted as Field's bodyguard, and was a real thug, or so I thought. He occasionally came to the office. As for anything else about him, I can't remember, officer.\" [1190] \"Of course, he knew you?\" Quinn asked. [1191] \"Ah\u2014I think he should, \"Morgan answered somewhat uncertainly. \"I've never spoken to him, but he must have seen me when he came to the office.\" [1192] \"Well, okay, Morgan,\" Quine muttered as he stood up. \"This has been a very interesting chat, and I've learned a lot. And\u2014never mind, I guess that's all for now. Keep doing what you're doing, Morgan, but stay in the city\u2014in case we need to find you. Remember this, okay?\" [1193] \"I won't forget,\" Morgan said listlessly. \"And\u2014of course, what I told you about my son\u2014won't be known, will it?\" [1194] \"You don't have to worry about that, Morgan,\" Quine said. A few minutes later, Quine and Ellery walked onto the sidewalk. [1195] \"So, it's blackmail, father,\" Ellery whispered. \"You know, this makes me think of something, do you?\" [1196] \"Well, son, I have some ideas too!\" Quinn chuckled. The two of them walked briskly in the direction of headquarters without exchanging a word. [1197] Chapter 12: The Quinn Father and Son Attend a Social Event\n[1198] On Wednesday morning, in the Quinn household, Juno was pouring coffee for the dazed officer and the chattering Ellery when the phone rang. Both Ellery and his father jumped up to answer the phone. [1199] \"Hey! What are you doing?\" Quinn called out, \"I was waiting for the phone, it's probably for me.\" [1200] \"All right, all right, sir, let a book lover use his phone first,\" Ellery retorted, \"I have a feeling it's my friend, the book dealer, calling about that hard-to-find Faulkner novel.\" [1201] \"Ellery, don't\u2014\u2014\" As they bantered playfully across the table, Junna picked up the phone. [1202] \"Detective\u2014is it for the detective? Detective, \" Junna held the receiver tightly against his chest and grinned, \"It's for you.\" [1203] Ellery slumped into his chair, and Quinn, with a smug look, picked up the phone. [1204] \"Hello?\" [1205] \"This is Stoutz, calling from Field's office, Detective,\" a young, energetic, and enthusiastic voice came through the phone, \"Mr. Croning wants to speak with you.\" [1206] The officer frowned thoughtfully. Ellery listened intently, and even Juno's angular face showed impatient eagerness, standing motionless in the corner, as if waiting for important news. Juno was similar to his primate relatives in this respect\u2014always alert and curious, which always made the Quin family happy. [1207] Finally, a high-pitched voice came over the phone. \"This is Tim Croning, Officer,\" he said, \"How have you been? I haven't had time to visit you in a while.\" [1208] \"I'm a bit hunched over now, and not as spry as I used to be, Tim. But, anyway, I'm still alive.\" Quin replied, \"What's up? Any new developments?\" [1209] \"This is the strangest part of the whole case, Officer,\" Croning's voice came over the phone, excited, \"You know, I've been watching Field for years. He's been my nightmare. The district attorney told me he already spoke to you about this, so I won't go into detail. But after all these years of surveillance, waiting, and investigation, I haven't found a single piece of evidence that could put that scoundrel in court. He's a villain, Officer\u2014I'd bet my life on it... Anyway, it's old news. I know Field well, and I shouldn't hold out any hope for him. However\u2014um, I can't help but pray that he'll slip up in some way, somehow, that we can get our hands on his secret files and bring him to justice. But, Officer\u2014I've made no progress.\" [1210] A flash of disappointment crossed Quin's face. Ellery saw it and sighed, getting up to pace restlessly around the room. [1211] \"I suppose that's just the way it goes, Tim,\" Quin replied sincerely, \"Don't worry - we'll find another way.\" [1212] \"Officer,\" Cronin suddenly said, \"You've got too much on your plate. Field is a slippery character. And, in my opinion, the genius who managed to evade Field's bodyguards and kill him is also slippery - definitely. By the way, we've only gone through half the files. Maybe the stuff we've looked at won't be as disappointing as I said. There's a lot in there that suggests Field has been up to no good - just no direct evidence of his crimes. I hope we'll find something more as we continue checking.\" [1213] \"Alright, Tim - keep checking,\" Quin grumbled, \"Let me know if you find anything... Is Loon around?\" [1214] \"You mean the office manager?\" Cronin whispered, \"He's around here. What's up?\" [1215] \"Keep a close eye on him,\" Quin said, \"I have a hunch he's not as dumb as he seems. Don't let him get too close to those files. We've heard he might be involved in Field's side business.\" [1216] \"Understood, officer. I'll call you back later.\" The phone clicked as Croning hung up. [1217] At 10:30, Quinn and Ellery pushed open the high doors of the entrance to the Eves-Pop residence on the Embankment Road. Ellery remarked that the atmosphere was quite suitable for inviting people in formal attire, and he felt quite uncomfortable as he walked through the stone door. [1218] In fact, the mansion that housed the Eves-Pop family's destiny, which was irregular in layout and made of large old stones, stood on a vast lawn far from the road. \"Must have cost a lot of money,\" the officer murmured as he looked around the sprawling lawn. The high iron fence around the house was followed by a garden, a summerhouse, walking paths, and pavilions - it was hard to believe that one was still in the city. The Eves-Pop family was wealthy, and this property, which was not rare, could be traced back to the colonial period of America. [1219] An elderly gentleman with a full beard and a noble air opened the front door. His back was as straight as a ramrod, and he looked down his nose at the world. Ellery stood lazily in the doorway, admiring the gentleman in his uniform. Quinn fumbled in his pocket for a business card. He searched for a long time but couldn't find one, and the statue-like butler stood there. Finally, a red-faced Quinn found a worn-out business card and placed it on the butler's outstretched tray. He watched the butler turn and return to the dark room. [1220] When Franklin Eves-Pop appeared in a wide doorway with a carved decoration, Quinn straightened up, and Ellery smiled at his father. [1221] The financier hurried towards them. \"Officer! Mr. Quinn!\" he exclaimed. \"Welcome! Did you have to wait long?\" [1222] The officer gave a vague greeting, and they passed through the high-ceilinged, well-polished hall, which contained simple, old wooden furniture. [1223] \"You're punctual, gentlemen,\" said Eves-Pope, standing aside and ushering Quinn and his son into a large room. [1224] \"There are a few more people attending our little meeting. I'm sure you'll recognize everyone here.\" [1225] \"Financialer, quickly towards them. \"Officer! Mr. Quinn!\" he exclaimed. \"Welcome! Did you have to wait long?\" The officer gave a vague greeting, and they passed through the high-ceilinged, well-polished hall, which contained simple, old wooden furniture. \"You're punctual, gentlemen,\" said Eves-Pope, standing aside and ushering Quinn and his son into a large room. \"There are a few more people attending our little meeting. I'm sure you'll recognize everyone here.\" [1226] Officer Quinn and Ellery looked around. \"I know everyone, sir, except for that gentleman \u2013 I believe he is Mr. Stanford Eves-Pope,\" said Mr. Quinn. \"My son still needs to meet everyone \u2013 Mr. Pear, right? \u2013 Mr. Barry \u2013 of course, and Mr. Eves-Pope.\" [1227] The introductions were somewhat awkward. \"Ha, Quinn!\" said the district attorney Sampson, hurrying across the room. \"I couldn't miss this, I said to myself, 'to be at this investigation, most of the people attending are people I've never seen before.'\" [1228] \"What's Pear doing here?\" Quinn whispered to the district attorney. Meanwhile, Ellery joined the conversation with the three young people on the other side of the room. Mr. Eves-Pope excused himself and left. [1229] \"He's Stanford Eves-Pope's friend, of course, and he's also close to Barry,\" said the district attorney. \"Before you arrived, I overheard them talking, and it was Stanford, Eves-Pope's son, who introduced him to his sister Frances. That's how she met Barry and fell in love. Pear seems to be getting along well with the young lady.\" [1230] \"I'm curious to know how the Eves-Popes, with their aristocratic background, view their children's relationships with these middle-class companions,\" said the officer, looking over at the small group of people on the other side of the room. [1231] \"You'll find out soon enough,\" Sampson chuckled. \"Just pay attention to how cold Eves-Pop's wife is every time she sees any of these actors. You can imagine the reception they would get from a group of Bolsheviks.\" [1232] Quine stood with his hands behind his back, curiously surveying the room. It was a study, filled with an impressive collection of books, all meticulously categorized and displayed behind gleaming glass. The center of the room held only a table. The police officer appreciated that for a millionaire, the study was surprisingly understated. [1233] \"By the way,\" Sampson continued. \"Eve Ellis is here, the lady who was with Eves-Pop's daughter and her fianc\u00e9 at the Roman Theater on Monday night. You mentioned her. I believe she's upstairs, keeping the young heiress company. Don't think Eves-Pop's wife likes it, but they're both very charming girls.\" [1234] \"What a pleasant place this would be for Eves-Pop to entertain these actors privately,\" said Quine. [1235] The four young people approached Quine and Ellery. Stanford was a tall, well-groomed young man, with a stylish outfit and dark circles under his eyes. Quine immediately noticed his agitation. Pile and Barry, the two actors, were impeccably dressed. [1236] \"Quin told me you're dealing with a difficult case, Inspector,\" said Stanford Eves-Pope in a loud voice. \"We're all very sorry that my sister is involved. How did her wallet end up in that man's pocket? I assure you, Barry has been up for days on end worrying about Francis.\" [1237] \"My dear young man,\" said the detective, his eyes flashing, \"if I knew how Miss Eves-Pope's wallet ended up in Monte Field's pocket, I wouldn't have come here this morning. That's one of the interesting things about this case.\" [1238] \"You find it interesting, Inspector, but we don't,\" said Stanford. \"You surely don't think Francis has anything to do with this case, do you?\" [1239] Quin smiled slightly. \"It's too early to say,\" he countered. \"I haven't heard Miss Eves-Pope's version of events yet.\" [1240] \"She'll explain everything, Inspector,\" said Stephen Barry, his handsome face weary. \"You needn't worry about that. She's been under this ridiculous suspicion, and I'm furious about it\u2014the whole thing is absurd!\" [1241] \"I can certainly understand your feelings, Mr. Barry,\" the officer said kindly, \"I wish to apologize for my behavior last night. I may have been too \u2013 harsh.\" [1242] \"I apologize as well, officer,\" Barry replied with a pale smile, \"I suppose what I said in the office was not meant, just a moment of excitement \u2013 seeing Francis \u2013 Miss Evans-Poppelwell faint \u2013\" he was somewhat embarrassed and did not continue. [1243] The burly, ruddy-faced Pierce wore morning dress and put his arm affectionately around Barry's shoulder. \"I am sure the officer will understand, Stephen, my old friend,\" he said cheerfully, \"do not take it to heart \u2013 everything will come out in the end.\" [1244] \"You can trust Quinn, officer,\" Sampson said, elbowing Quinn playfully, \"he is the only detective I have ever seen with a badge and a heart of gold \u2013 if Miss Evans-Poppelwell can clear this up to his satisfaction, with a reasonable explanation, the matter will be settled.\" [1245] \"Oh, I cannot say for certain,\" Ellery thought aloud, \"father always brings surprises. As for Miss Evans-Poppelwell \u2013\" he smiled sympathetically and bowed to the actress, \"Mr. Barry, you are indeed fortunate.\" [1246] \"You won't think that when you meet my mother,\" Stanford Eves-Pope said slowly, \"I believe she's already here.\" [1247] The men turned towards the door. A very fat woman swayed in, with a nurse holding onto her bulky arm with one hand and a green bottle in the other. The financier followed briskly, with a young-looking man with a full head of white hair by his side. He wore a dark suit and carried a black bag. [1248] \"Katherine, my dear,\" Eves-Pope said in a low voice as the fat woman sat down in a large chair, \"these two gentlemen are the ones I told you about \u2013 Detective Richard Quinns and Ellery Quinns.\" [1249] The Quinns bowed. Mrs. Eves-Pope was nearsighted and glanced at them coldly. \"Very nice to meet you, I'm sure,\" she said shrilly, \"where's the nurse? I feel dizzy.\" [1250] The uniformed girl rushed to her side, with the green bottle ready. Mrs. Eves-Pope closed her eyes, took a sip, and sighed deeply. The financier quickly called the white-haired man, Dr. Vinson Cunningham, their family doctor. The doctor rushed over and disappeared behind the butler. \"Quite a guy, this Cunningham,\" Sampson whispered to Quinns, \"not only the most popular doctor on this street, but also a genius scientist.\" The officer raised his eyebrows but said nothing. [1251] \"I don't like the medical profession, and that's one of the reasons why my mother is like that,\" Stanford Evans-Pope whispered loudly to Ellery. [1252] \"Ah! Frances, my dear!\" Evans-Pope rushed forward, followed by Barry, towards the door. Mrs. Evans-Pope stared coldly at Barry's back with an expression of disdain. James Peel coughed awkwardly and muttered something to Sampson. [1253] Frances entered the room, wearing a thin morning gown and looking pale and weary. She greeted the officer with a faint smile and introduced Eve Ellis, the actress. The two girls sat next to Mrs. Evans-Pope. The old lady sat upright in her chair, like a mother lion watching over her cubs. Two servants quietly entered the room and placed chairs for the men. With much insistence from Evans-Pope, Quin sat in a large chair. Ellery did not sit down, but leaned against the bookshelf behind him, facing away from everyone. [1254] The conversation stopped, and the officer cleared his throat, turning to Frances. She blinked a few times, then met Quin's gaze with calmness. [1255] \"First, Miss Francis - I hope I may call you that,\" Quin said in a fatherly tone, \"Allow me to explain the way I questioned you on Monday evening. I'm sure it seemed too harsh to you, and I apologize. Evans-Pope told me you could explain your actions on the night of the murder at Monte Field. Therefore, I thought, in your case, after this morning's conversation, we could eliminate you from the list of suspects. Before we begin, please believe what I said on Monday evening - you are just one of many suspects. In such a situation, I usually do things by habit. Now, I know that, for a girl of your background and social status, accepting questioning from the police in such a tense situation would be quite shocking and cause you to be in your current state.\" [1256] Francis smiled wearily. \"I don't blame you, officer,\" she whispered clearly, \"it was my fault, I was foolish. I'm ready to answer any questions you have.\" [1257] \"Wait a moment, my dear,\" the officer turned slightly and addressed everyone quietly in the room, \"I have something to clarify, gentlemen and ladies,\" he said seriously, \"we are gathered here for a clear purpose. We know that Eves-Pope's purse was found in the victim's pocket, and she was unable to explain why that happened. Our goal is to find a possible connection between these two events - of course, there will be a connection. Now, regardless of the results of this morning's conversation, I must ask you, everything said here must be kept confidential. As District Attorney Sampson knows, I usually do not conduct investigations in front of so many people. Today is an exception, and I am doing this because I believe that you all deeply care about this girl who has unfortunately been involved in this case. But if today's conversation is known to outsiders, I will not hesitate to be ruthless. Is that clear to everyone?\" [1258] \"I say, officer,\" Little Eves-Pope protested, \"that's a bit too much, don't you think?\" [1259] \"Perhaps so, Little Eves-Pope, sir,\" the officer countered with a cold smile, \"that's why I agreed to let you all be present.\" [1260] There was a rustling sound, and Eves-Pope's wife opened her mouth as if to angrily express her opinion. But seeing her husband's stern look, she closed her mouth and swallowed her protest. She glanced at the actress sitting next to Francis. Eve Ellis's face flushed. The nurse standing next to Eves-Pope's wife held a smelling salts bottle, like a set dog on standby. [1261] \"Now, Miss Francis,\" Quin continued in a kindly tone, \"Here's the situation: I examined the body of a man named Monte Field, a well-known lawyer, who was clearly enjoying an interesting play when he was suddenly killed.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBut in fact, in this sordid affair, it was my son Ellery who showed his cleverness. He's a smart kid.\" [2137] The officer sniffed his snuff and leaned comfortably in his armchair, Sampson and Cronin relaxed, and Juno curled up in the corner, listening intently. [2138] \"Looking back on the Field case,\" the officer began, \"I sometimes have to mention Benjamin Morgan, who was truly the most innocent victim of all. I want you to remember, Henry, whatever I say about Morgan, that's it. Don't say anything, either at work or in social circles. I've already made Tim promise to keep it secret... \"\n[2139] The two men nodded silently. The officer continued, \"Most criminal investigations begin by looking for a motive, which goes without saying. Many times, once you know the reason behind the crime, you can eliminate the suspects one by one. In this case, the motive has been unclear for a long time. Although there were some signs, such as Benjamin Morgan's secrets, they were not conclusive. Morgan had been blackmailed by Field for a long time - gentlemen, although you know Field's other social habits, you may not know some of his activities. This seems to suggest that blackmail was a possible motive - or to get rid of blackmail. However, many things could also become motives - for example, revenge by a criminal who was 'sent to prison' by Field; or by a member of his criminal gang. Field had many enemies, of course, and also many friends, but only because he held their leashes. Many people - both men and women - had a motive to kill this lawyer. That night at the Roman Theater, we had too many urgent things to consider and solve, so we didn't bother with the motive. It was always lurking behind the scenes, waiting to appear. [2140] \"But please note this: if this were blackmail - as Ellery and I eventually concluded, because this possibility seemed the greatest - then at least this can be said: Field must have had some revealing documents in his possession. We know that Morgan's documents existed. Cronin insisted that the documents he was looking for were somewhere. So we had to stay alert and look for documents - tangible evidence that might shed light on the underlying situation of the crime.\" [2141] \"Meanwhile, in terms of documents, Ellery noticed that there were many books on handwriting analysis in Field's belongings. We can conclude that, as we know, Field had blackmailed (for example, in the Morgan case) and had a suspicion of multiple cases; coupled with his strong interest in handwriting analysis, a person like him would likely forge handwriting. If so, and if it can be explained, it may mean that Field had the habit of forging the original blackmail documents. He did so, of course, to sell the forgeries and retain the originals for further blackmail. His connections with the underworld undoubtedly made him familiar with the tricks of the trade. Later, we found that this assumption was correct. At that time, we determined that blackmail was the motive for this crime. But remember, this led us astray, because any suspect could have been the victim of blackmail, and we did not know who it was. [2142] The officer frowned and adjusted his posture. [2143] \"But I handled this explanation wrongly, which shows how habits can control a person. I always start with the motive\u2026 but in the investigation, the only important, core thing that was visible was a jumbled mess of clues \u2013 or rather, the lack of clues. I mean the lost hat\u2026\n[2144] \"Unfortunately, on Monday night at the Roman Theater, we were in such a hurry to question immediately that we overlooked the significance of the lost hat. It's not that we weren't puzzled by it at first \u2013 far from it, it was one of the first things I noticed when I examined the body. As for Ellery, he noticed it when he bent down to check the body. But what could we do? We had hundreds of details to deal with \u2013 questions to ask, orders to give, contradictions and suspicious discoveries to sort out \u2013 so, as I said, we missed a major opportunity due to negligence. If we had analyzed the significance of the missing hat at the time \u2013 perhaps the case would have been solved that night. [2145] \"Well, it didn't take too long, so don't complain,\" Sampson laughed. \"It's only been nine days since the murder \u2013 what's there to complain about?\" [2146] The detective shrugged. \"There's a big difference,\" he said. \"If we had been able to deduce it at the time - well! When we finally took the time to thoroughly analyze the hat issue, the first thing we had to ask ourselves was: why would the hat be taken? There seem to be only two answers that hold up: one, the hat itself was evidence in the case; two, there was something the killer wanted in the hat, so he took the risk. It turned out that both answers were correct. The hat was evidence in the case, as the name \"Stephen Barry\" was written underneath the sweatband inside, in indelible ink; and the hat contained something the killer desperately wanted - the blackmail document. Of course, he thought it was the original at the time. [2147] \"This didn't help us much, but it was a starting point. When we ordered the theater closed and left on Monday night, despite a thorough search, we still couldn't find the missing hat. However, we also didn't know how the hat left the theater - whether it left in some mysterious way or if it was still in the theater but not found. On Thursday morning, when we returned to the theater, we finally confirmed the whereabouts of Monte Field's bothersome hat - that is, it was negative. It wasn't in the theater. Since the theater had been sealed since Monday night, it could be inferred that the hat was taken away on the night of the crime. [2148] \"And everyone left the theater on Monday night with only one hat on. Therefore, based on the second search, we can conclude that someone left the theater with or wearing Monte Field's hat that night, so he must have left his own hat in the theater. [2149] \"Unless he and the other audience members were allowed to leave and slipped away, he couldn't have taken the hat out of the theater; because all the exits were guarded or locked at that time. The left inner alley had Jesse Lynch and Eleanor Libby blocking it, followed by usher John Tice watching, and then one of our police officers guarding. There was no exit on the right, so the hat couldn't be taken from there. [2150] \"Following this line of thought - because Field's hat was a top hat, and no one leaves a theater wearing a top hat without a full evening gown - we observed this very carefully - so whoever took the\n[2151] \"It seems very logical,\" Sampson admitted, \"although it is quite complex.\" [2152] \"You have no idea how complicated this is,\" the officer said solemnly, \"because we must remember other possibilities - for example, the person who took Field's hat is not the killer, but an accomplice. Let's continue. [2153] \"The next question we ask ourselves is: What happened to the high top hat left by the murderer in the theater? How did he dispose of it? Where did he put it?... I can tell you, it's a puzzle. We searched the place up and down. Yes, we found some hats in the backstage, and Mrs. Philip, the custodian, confirmed that they were personal belongings of the actors, but none of them belonged to them privately. So, where could the high top hat left by the murderer be? Ellery hit the truth with his sharp mind. He muttered to himself: 'The murderer's hat must be here. We didn't find any unusual or unremarkable high top hats, so the high top hats we searched must not be unremarkable.' That's the crux of the matter, isn't it? Simple to the point of absurdity, yet I didn't think of it.\" [2154] \"What kind of hat is this? - An ordinary hat in an ordinary place, so unremarkable that no one questioned it. At the Roman Theater, all costumes were rented from Le Brun, so the answer is simple: it's a rented hat for the performance. Where would these hats be? Either in the men's dressing room or in the wardrobe room where all the actors' costumes are stored. When Ellery reasoned this out, he took Mrs. Philip to the backstage, checked every hat in the men's dressing room and the wardrobe room. Every single hat there \u2013 all of them accounted for, none missing \u2013 was a prop hat with Le Brun's label on the lining. And Field's hat, we had all confirmed, was a \"Brown Brothers\" derby, not among the props, and not anywhere backstage.\" [2155] \"Since no one left the theater wearing more than one hat on Monday night, and since Field's hat was undoubtedly taken the same evening, it can be concluded that the murderer's own top hat must have been in the Roman Theater during the closure period and was still there when we conducted our second search. Now, the hats left in the theater are all props. Therefore, the next inference is that the murderer left wearing Field's hat, so his own hat must have been one of the props in the backstage. So, to reiterate, these prop hats are the only actual top hats that exist.\" [2156] \"In other words - one of the props backstage belonged to the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2157] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2158] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2159] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2160] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2161] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2162] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2163] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2164] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2165] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2166] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2167] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2168] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2169] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2170] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2171] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2172] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2173] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of 