Field, [2194] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2195] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2196] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2197] the person who left the theater on Monday night wearing a full suit and a silk hat of Field, [2198]\n[2157] \"If this person is the killer \u2013 it couldn't be anyone else \u2013 then our search area is greatly reduced. He could only be one of the male actors wearing evening attire who left, or someone closely associated with the theater and dressed similarly. This person would first need to have a prop hat that must be left behind; secondly, have access to the costume storage room and dressing room without being detected; and finally, have the opportunity to leave his prop hat in the costume storage room and dressing room. [2158] \"Now let's analyze the possibility of the second scenario - the killer is closely related to the theater but not an actor.\" The policeman paused, taking a deep breath of his cherished snuff, \"The backstage staff can be ruled out as none of them wore evening gowns, which are necessary to take away Field's high hat. Similarly, the cashier, usher, doorman, and other minor staff can also be ruled out. The promoter Harry Nelson was dressed in ordinary civilian clothes. Manager Poulson wore a full suit, yes, but I measured his hat size and found it to be six and three-quarters - an unusually small size. He couldn't wear Field's hat, which was seven and three-quarters. We left the theater before him, that's right. But on my way out, I specifically instructed Thomas Wiley to search Poulson as thoroughly as he would search others. Earlier that evening, I checked Poulson's hat in his office out of a sense of responsibility and found it to be a round top hat. Wiley later reported that Poulson left with this top hat, without taking any other hats. Now - if Poulson were the person we were looking for, he might have left with Field's hat, although it was too big for him to wear, but he could have carried it. However, he left with a top hat, so there is no doubt that he could not have taken Field's hat, as the door was locked when he left the theater, and no one - I can guarantee with my staff on duty - entered the theater until Thursday morning when I opened the door myself. Theoretically, anyone who could hide Field's hat in the theater, such as Poulson or any other employee, could be the killer. But this last assumption was refuted by the report of our official building expert, Edmund Crow. He clearly stated that there are no secret hiding places in the Roman Theater. [2159] \"Having eliminated Parelli, Nelsen, and the staff, there was only the possibility of the actors left. I'll leave it to you to guess how we narrowed down the field to Barry. The most intriguing part of this case, in fact, was a series of surprising and intricate deductions that led to the truth. I should say 'we' \u2014 but really, I should say 'Eliot'\u2026\"\n[2160] \"As a detective, you are quite the bashful one,\" Crooning chuckled, \"Oh, this is more thrilling than a detective story. I should be working now, but my boss seems equally interested \u2013 go on, officer!\" [2161] Quinn smiled and continued speaking. [2162] \"The fact that the killer's identity was traced to an actor,\" he continued, \"answered a question that may have occurred to you and has been bothering us from the start. At first, we didn't understand why the theater was chosen as the meeting place for the secret deal. You stop to think, and in general, the theater has many inconveniences. Just one thing: to ensure privacy, you have to buy several extra tickets around the seats to make them empty. Compared to other more convenient meeting places, choosing such a chaotic place is simply ridiculous! The theater is mostly dark and quiet, which is unsettling. Any unexpected sound or conversation would be noticed. The audience is also a potential danger \u2013 they could be recognized. However, when you realize that Barry is an actor, all these problems are solved. From his perspective, the theater is ideal \u2013 because when the victim is found dead in the front row of the stalls, who would suspect the actor as the killer? Of course, Field consented to this arrangement, not realizing what Barry was thinking, and not realizing that he was conspiring in his own death. You must remember, he dealt with dangerous people all the time, even if he had some suspicions, he thought he could handle it. This might have made him a little too confident \u2013 of course, we'll never know. [2163] \"Let's go back to Ellery - my favorite topic,\" the detective continued, chuckling as usual, \"Besides these deductions about the hat - actually, before these deductions appeared - Ellery had noticed suspicious signs at the Evans-Pope house during their meeting. It was clear that during the intermission, Field had approached Frances Evans-Pope in the alley not just for flirting. In Ellery's view, there was some kind of connection between these two distant individuals. However, this did not mean that Frances had to know about this connection. She was absolutely certain that she had never heard of or seen Field before. We have no reason to suspect her, but we have ample reason to believe her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThey lifted the seats; the officer surreptitiously took out a long needle from his chest pocket, tentatively poking it into the plush cushion, then knelt down to examine every inch of the carpet with his flashlight. [1463] The two detectives had rolled up the waterproof canvas cover and, under the officer's brief instructions, began to search the boxes, each responsible for one side of the theater. [1464] After a while, the four of them were working in silence, only the officer's labored breathing breaking the stillness. Ellery's work was very efficient, while the old man was slower. After completing a row of search, they met in the middle, exchanged meaningful glances, shook their heads, and continued. [1465] After Poulzer left about twenty minutes ago, the focused officers and Ellery were startled by the ringing of the phone. The sharp and clear ring echoed through the quiet theater. The father and son looked at each other blankly for a moment, then the old man chuckled and hobbled towards Poulzer's office. [1466] Soon, he returned with a smile. \"It's Poulson,\" he said. \"He went to Field's office, but it was locked. No wonder \u2013 it's only 8:45. But I told him to wait until Cronin comes. Won't be long now.\" [1467] Ellery chuckled, and they resumed their work. [1468] Fifteen minutes later, just as the two were about to finish, the front door opened, and a short, middle-aged woman in black stood blinking in the bright arc lights. The officer stood up and went to greet her. [1469] \"Are you Mrs. Poulson?\" he called out kindly. \"It's so nice of you to come so quickly, ma'am. I'm sure you know me \u2013 I'm Quinn.\" [1470] Ellery stepped forward with a rare smile, bowing politely and sincerely. Mrs. Poulson was a likable old woman, short and kind-looking, with silver hair and a friendly demeanor. She immediately won over Quinn's affection for her charming middle-aged appearance, and he had a fondness for mature ladies. [1471] \"I know Mr. Quine,\" she said, extending her hand. \"He was very kind to an old lady on Monday night... I was afraid you'd have to wait for me, sir!\" She turned to the officer and said softly, \"Mr. Poulson sent a message this morning - you know I don't have a phone. I used to be an actress... so I came as soon as I could.\" [1472] The officer smiled broadly. \"That's quite a feat for a lady, Mrs. Philip,\" he said. [1473] \"My father kissed the ground at the feet of the Sphinx, Mrs. Philip,\" Ellery said gravely. \"Don't believe a word he says... I think you'll be able to handle the rest of the front row, Dad? I'd like to chat with Mrs. Philip for a while. Will you be all right with this work alone?\" [1474] \"Of course I will,\" the officer snorted. \"Leave the aisle open, son... Thank you very much for your help, Mrs. Philip.\" [1475] The silver-haired lady smiled. Ellery took her arm and led her to the stage. Quine watched them go, enviously, then shrugged and went back to searching. Soon, when he straightened up, he saw Ellery and Mrs. Philip talking earnestly on the stage, like two actors rehearsing. Quine continued to slowly walk between the rows of seats, looking for clues, but he was still empty-handed when he looked up again. The stage was empty, and Ellery and the old lady were nowhere to be found. [1476] Detective Quinn finally arrived at seat LL32 on the left - it was here that Monte Field met his untimely demise. He carefully examined the cushion, his eyes full of disappointment. He slowly walked through the carpet at the back of the theater, entered Pincus's office. He reappeared shortly, heading towards Harry Nelson's cubicle, the publicity manager. He stayed in the cubicle for a while, then emerged and entered the cashier's office. After closing the door, he descended the stairs on the right side of the theater, leading to the main floor's general lounge beneath the front row seats. He spent some time checking every corner, every recess in the wall, every trash can - he found nothing in any of them. He looked at the water dispenser's large container below with a questioning gaze, then peered inside, finding nothing. He sighed and opened the door with the gold lettering \"Women's Restroom\" and went in. After a while, he emerged and pushed open the double-spring door of the \"Men's Restroom\" and walked in. [1477] After thoroughly checking the lower level, he dragged his heavy steps back up the stairs. In the front row, he found Louis Pincus waiting, his face flushed with fatigue but wearing a victorious smile. The short manager held a package wrapped in brown paper. [1478] \"Did you see Cronin, Pincus?\" Detective Quinn rushed forward. \"You're a real pal, you really are, I can't thank you enough. Is this the package Cronin gave you?\" [1479] \"Yes, Cronin is a good man. He came with two others named Stotts and Loyn shortly after I called him. I waited less than ten minutes. I guess it's important, right, officer?\" [1480] \"Important?\" Detective Quinn said, taking the package from the manager. \"You have no idea how important it is! I'll tell you all about it later... Can I leave you for a moment, Pincus?\" [1481] The small manager's eyes flashed a moment of disappointment, but he nodded. The detective smiled and retreated to a dark corner. Pencroft shrugged and entered his own office. [1482] He left his hat and coat in the office and emerged as the detective was slipping the package into his pocket. [1483] \"Did you get what you wanted, sir?\" Pencroft asked. [1484] \"Oh, yes, not a moment too soon!\" the detective said, rubbing his hands together. \"Now - since Ellery still hasn't shown up - I thought I'd wait in your office until he gets back.\" [1485] They entered Pencroft's private room and sat down. The manager lit a long Turkish cigarette, and the detective produced a snuff box. [1486] \"If I may ask, sir,\" Pendergast said, crossing his short, fat legs and puffing out a cloud of smoke, \"how is the case going?\" [1487] Quin shook his head regretfully. \"Not well\u2014not well. We seem to be making no progress on the main points of the case. In fact, I'll be frank with you, unless we find a lead on something, we're going to fail\u2014it's been a very difficult investigation. He closed the snuff box with a sigh. [1488] \"Too bad, sir,\" Pendergast said sympathetically, licking his lips. \"I had hoped\u2014ah, never mind! I suppose we can't let personal matters interfere with the requirements of justice. If you don't mind telling me, sir, what are you looking for?\" [1489] The policeman smiled. \"Not at all. You've been such a great help this morning, and\u2014oh, I'm an idiot, I should have thought of it before! How long have you been managing the Roman Theater, Pendergast?\" [1490] The manager raised his eyebrows. \"Since it opened,\" he said. \"Before that, I managed the old Electra Theater on 43rd Street\u2014it belonged to Gordon Davis.\" He explained. [1491] \"Oh!\" The officer seemed to be lost in thought. \"So you must be very familiar with the theatre\u2014perhaps you're as familiar with its construction as the architect?\" [1492] \"I am quite familiar with it, yes.\" Pincus admitted, leaning back in his chair. [1493] \"Wonderful! So, here's a little question for you, Pincus... Suppose you wanted to hide something\u2014say, a large hat\u2014somewhere in the theatre, even if you turned the place upside down, you wouldn't find it. What would you do? Where would you hide it?\" [1494] Pincus frowned, pondering his cigarette. \"An unusual question, officer,\" he finally said. \"I'm not sure how to answer. I'm very familiar with the floor plan; I've discussed it with the architect before the theatre was built, and he even sought my input. I can think of many places to hide something as small as a hat, but none of them would withstand a thorough search.\" [1495] \"Understood.\" The officer glanced at his nails, a hint of disappointment on his face. \"So, it's no use. You know, we've searched the whole place top to bottom, and still found no trace...\"\n[1496] The door opened, and Ellery walked in, a little dirty. The officer glanced at him curiously and eagerly. Van Dusen hesitated, clearly wanting the father and son to be alone. The Quinns exchanged a knowing glance. [1497] \"It's okay, Van Dusen \u2013 don't leave,\" said the officer firmly. \"We have no secrets from you. Sit down, old man!\" [1498] Van Dusen sat down. [1499] \"You don't think, Dad,\" said Ellery, sitting at the table and adjusting his glasses, \"that it's time to inform Mr. Van Dusen that the theater can open tonight, as we had decided when he left? Remember, we decided that the theater could open to the public and resume normal performances tonight... \"\n[1500] \"How could I forget?\" said the officer, not blinking an eye, despite hearing this fabricated decision for the first time. \"I think we'll be lifting the ban on the theater soon, Van Dusen. We haven't found anything here, so there's no reason to interrupt your income. You can organize tonight's performance \u2013 in fact, we're eager to see a play, aren't we, Ellery?\" [1501] \"Not quite,\" Ellery said, lighting a cigarette, \"we insist on seeing it.\" [1502] \"Indeed,\" the detective said sternly, \"we insist on seeing it, Poulzer.\" [1503] The manager jumped up from his chair, beaming. \"That's great news, gentlemen!\" he exclaimed, \"I'll call Mr. Davis right away and tell him the good news. Of course -\" his face fell, \"it's too late to expect any reaction from the public tonight. This last-minute notice...\u201d\n[1504] \"You don't have to worry, Poulzer,\" the detective countered, \"I'm the one who closed your door, and I'll make sure the theater is compensated tonight. I'll call the newspaper people and have them advertise the opening in the next edition. That means you'll get unexpected publicity. Without a doubt, it's free advertising, along with the public's natural curiosity, your tickets will sell out.\" [1505] \"You really do things fairly, detective,\" Poulzer said, rubbing his hands together, \"is there anything else I can do for you?\" [1506] \"You forgot one thing, dad,\" Ellery interjected, turning to the dark-skinned, short statured manager, \"can you ensure that the tickets to the left of LL32 and LL30 are not sold tonight? The officer and I want to attend the performance. You know, we haven't enjoyed this pleasure yet. Needless to say, we want to remain anonymous, Mr. Pensler - we don't like the audience's applause and the like. Of course, you will act secretly.\" [1507] \"As you wish, Mr. Queene. I'll have the cashier leave those two tickets,\" Mr. Pensler said, delighted, \"Now, officer - didn't you say you were going to call the newspaper?\" [1508] \"Certainly,\" Queene picked up the phone and spoke briefly with the economic news editor of several city newspapers. After he finished the call, Mr. Pensler bid them farewell and went off to make his own arrangements. [1509] Queene Officer and his son strolled out and took their seats in the front row of the auditorium, where Flint and the two detectives checking the boxes were waiting for them. [1510] \"You stay here as instructed,\" the officer ordered, \"especially today afternoon. Did anyone find anything?\" [1511] Flint frowned. \"I should have gone clam digging in the Nantucket Islands,\" he grumbled, \"I didn't do well on Monday night, and I'm not expecting to find anything today either. The place upstairs was cleaned like a dog's teeth. I think I'll go back and check again.\" [1512] Queene patted the shoulder of the tall detective. \"What's wrong? Don't be like a child, boy. What can you find when there's nothing to find? Did you find anything?\" he asked the other two. [1513] They shook their heads dejectedly. [1514] After a while, the officer and Ellery got into a passing taxi and headed to their headquarters, which was not far away. The old man carefully closed the glass sliding window that separated the driver from the back seat. [1515] \"Hey, son,\" he turned to Ellery, who was lost in thought and smoking, \"explain to your old man what you did in Mr. Pausch's office!\" [1516] Ellery clenched his lips. He stared out the window for a moment before answering. \"Well, let's say this,\" he finally spoke. \"You didn't find anything in today's search, and neither did your men. I've searched carefully too, but I didn't find anything either. Dad, face it: Monte Field was wearing a hat when he went to see 'The Gunfight' on Monday night, and someone saw him wearing it when the second act began. Perhaps the killer took the hat with him when he left, because it's not in the Rome Theater now, and it hasn't been there since Monday night. I'll continue.\" [1517] \"Go on,\" the detective ordered. [1518] \"The second point is simple, even for a child. But allow me to offend your intelligence, Mr. Quinn... If the hat is not in the Rome Theater now, and it hasn't been there since Monday night, then it must have been taken out of the Rome Theater that evening!\" [1519] He paused, looking out the window thoughtfully. A traffic cop was waving his arms at the intersection of 42nd Street and Broadway. [1520] \"So, for three days, we've been exhausted, and we've finally established an important factual basis,\" he continued softly. \"That is, the hat we're looking for has left the Rome Theater... Indeed, it left the Rome Theater on the night of the murder. Now we face a bigger problem - how and when it left.\" He blew smoke and stared at the glowing end of his pipe. \"We know that no one went out wearing two hats or without a hat on Monday night, and no one left the theater disheveled. That is, no one wearing formal attire went out with a felt hat on, and no one wearing casual clothes went out with a silk top hat on. Remember, from this perspective, we didn't find anyone suspicious... Therefore, I conclude that the hat left the theater in the most natural way possible - that is, someone wearing appropriate clothing went out with it!\" [1521] He blew smoke and stared at the glowing end of his cigarette. \"We know that no one went out with two hats or without a hat on Monday evening, and no one left the theater disheveled. That is, no one wearing formal attire went out with a felt hat on, and no one wearing casual clothes went out with a silk top hat on. Remember, from this perspective, we have not found anyone suspicious... Therefore, I must conclude that the hat left the theater in the most natural way \u2013 that is, someone wearing appropriate clothing went out with it!\" [1522] The officer listened intently. He pondered Eretria's words for a moment before saying seriously, \"You've finally given us a lead, son. You said someone left the theater wearing Monte Field's hat \u2013 that's an important and insightful statement. But please answer this: How did he deal with the two hats? Because no one went out with two hats on.\" [1523] Eretria smiled. \"Now you've hit the core of the puzzle, dad. But let's not talk about that for now. We still have many points to ponder. For example, the person who left the theater wearing Monte Field's hat is one of two kinds of people: either he is the killer himself, or he is the accomplice of the killer.\" [1524] \"I understand what you mean,\" the officer mumbled. \"Continue.\" [1525] \"If he is the killer, we have established his gender and that he was wearing evening wear that night \u2013 perhaps not very insightful, as there were dozens of such people in the theater. If he is just an accomplice, then we must conclude \u2013 the killer has two possibilities: either he is a man wearing casual clothes who left wearing a top hat suspiciously, or he is a woman, so she could not possibly wear such a hat!\" [1526] He lowered his voice, and Ellery leaned in. They continued to whisper inaudibly until the taxi stopped in front of headquarters. [1527] Inspector Quin strode forward, and Ellery walked alongside him, as they walked through the dimly lit corridors. They reached his small office, and Inspector Wiley rose slowly. [1528] \"I thought you were lost, Inspector!\" he exclaimed. \"Stotsky was here not long ago, looking all grim. He said Croaning was furious in Field's office - they still couldn't find any evidence in the files.\" [1529] \"Shoo, shoo, Tom, my boy,\" the inspector whispered. \"I don't want to be bothered with trivial matters like locking up dead men. Ellery and I... \"\n[1530] The telephone rang. Quin strode forward, grabbed the receiver from the table. He listened, and the ruddy glow on his thin cheeks faded. His brow furrowed again. Ellery watched him with a strange expression. [1531] \"Officer?\" came a man's urgent voice. \"This is Haggstrom. I have something to report. Just a minute - I can't say too much. I've been tailing Angela Rathbone all morning... it's been quite a task... half an hour ago, she thought she'd shaken me - she got into a taxi and went downtown... and, uh, officer - three minutes ago, I saw her go into Benjamin Morgan's office!\" [1532] Quin barked, \"Keep a close eye on her when she comes out!\" Then he slammed the phone down. He turned slowly to Ellery and Wheeler, repeating Haggstrom's report. Ellery frowned thoughtfully. Wheeler seemed quite pleased. [1533] But the old man sat down weakly in his swivel chair, his voice a little unnatural. Finally, he murmured, \"What do you make of it?\" [1534] Chapter 15: Bringing Charges\n[1535] Detective Haggstrom was a man of calm demeanor. His ancestors had lived in the mountains of Norway, where sluggishness was considered a virtue and serene composure was highly valued.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:52 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nBy the way, the morgue's car should be outside \u2013 I called for one on the way here. Goodbye.\" Plautti yawned and left in a lackluster manner. [368] As soon as Dr. Plautti left, two attendants in white clothes rushed through the carpet with a stretcher. With Officer Quinn's signal, they lifted the motionless body and placed it on the stretcher, covered it with a blanket, and hurriedly carried it away. As the terrible burden was carried away, the agents and police standing by the door breathed a sigh of relief \u2013 the main work of the evening was basically done. The audience rustled, shifted, coughed, and murmured. The sudden departure of the body reignited their interest. [369] Officer Quinn turned to Ellery, sighing wearily. At that moment, there was an unsettling commotion at the back of the theater. The audience stood up from their seats and looked in the direction of the commotion, and the police officers shouted for them to be quiet. Officer Quinn quickly spoke a few words to a policeman standing beside him. Ellery moved aside, his eyes shining. The commotion grew louder, and soon two policemen appeared, dragging a struggling man. They dragged the man to the front of the left aisle, grabbed his arms tightly, and pushed him to his feet. [370] The man was short and had a rat-like appearance. He was dressed in cheap, drab clothing and a shabby black hat that a country clergyman might wear occasionally. His mouth twisted into a grotesque shape and occasionally spewed out vicious curses. However, as soon as he noticed Officer Quinn staring at him, he stopped struggling and hung his head. [371] \"We found this man trying to sneak out through the side door of the theater on the other side, sir,\" one of the policemen panted, not forgetting to push the man roughly. [372] Inspector Quin smiled and took a snuff box out of his pocket, took a deep breath, and habitually sneezed. He smiled at the man who was silent and shrinking between the two policemen. [373] \"Oh, oh, Parsons,\" he said kindly, \"you came just in time, and it's so good.\" [374] Chapter 4 Two Suspects\n[375] Some people are naturally unable to tolerate grumbling men. The crowd was silent under the control of the police, but the man called \"the priest\" was pathetically shouting. Ellery felt disgusted with this man's ridiculous behavior. [376] Erlie watched him with disgust as he noticed the man's ridiculous behavior. Detective Quin's words were clearly sarcastic, and the \"Minister\" stiffened and glared at the officer before trying to break free from the strong arms holding him. He twisted, spit, and cursed, but eventually fell silent. He stopped cursing, but his body was still writhing in anger, and the men holding him were twisting and turning as well. Another policeman joined the fray, pinning the man to the ground. Suddenly, the \"Minister\" deflated like a punctured balloon. A policeman roughly pulled him up, and he stood there, staring at the ground, holding his hat tightly. [377] Erlie turned away. [378] \"Come on, Parsons,\" Detective Quin continued, treating the man like a stubborn child who had just finished a tantrum and finally quieted down, \"you know, this won't work on me. Do you remember what happened last time in the River District? You played the same trick, and how did that turn out?\" [379] \"Ask and answer,\" a policeman growled, poking him in the ribs. [380] \"I know nothing, and I have nothing to say,\" the \"Minister\" mumbled, shifting his weight from one leg to the other. [381] \"I'm surprised at you, Parsons,\" Quinns officer said mildly, \"I haven't asked you what you know yet.\" [382] \"You have no right to arrest an innocent person!\" the \"priest\" shouted angrily, \"I'm as good as the others here, aren't I? I bought a ticket, and I paid with real money. Why won't you let me go home?\" [383] \"So, you bought a ticket, right?\" Quinns officer swayed his body and asked, \"Good, good, then dig out the ticket stub for old Quinns to see.\" [384] The \"priest\" mechanically reached into the pocket under his jacket, his fingers moving quickly inside. He slowly withdrew his hand, but there was nothing. He looked bewildered. He then began searching other pockets, with a look of regret on his face, which made Quinns officer laugh. [385] \"Damn it!\" the \"priest\" grumbled, \"I'm so unlucky. I usually keep the ticket stub, but tonight I threw it away. I'm sorry, officer.\" [386] \"No problem,\" Inspector Quinn said, his face growing stern. \"Enough with the act, delaying us! Kuzinelli, what were you doing at the theater tonight? Why did you suddenly slip away? Tell me.\" [387] The Reverend looked around. His arm was still firmly held by the police, and a group of serious-looking police officers surrounded him. He knew his chances of escape were slim. His expression changed again, feigning a sincere, indignant innocent. Tears blurred his small eyes, like a true Christian martyr, while Inspector Quinn and his men were like pagan persecutors. The Reverend often used this expression to prove his innocence, and it usually worked. [388] \"Officer,\" he said, \"you know, you have no right to question me like this, don't you, officer? Everyone has the right to a lawyer, right? Definitely has the right!\" [389] Inspector Quinn stared curiously at him. \"When was the last time you saw Field?\" he asked. [390] \"Field? You mean Monte Field? I've never heard of him, sir. What are you trying to pin on me?\" the Reverend whispered anxiously. \"What's the charge?\" [391] \"Nothing, Parsons, no charges will be filed. But if you don't want to answer now, I can let you cool off. Maybe you'll say something later... But Parsons, don't forget, there are still some small things to investigate in the Bonomo Silk robbery case.\" [392] Ellery was startled when he heard his father speak as \"the priest\" was being dragged towards the back of the theater. \"This 'priest' is really stupid, isn't he?\" said the officer. \"Trying to slip away like that...\"\n[393] \"Thanks to these little tricks,\" Ellery smiled, \"one mistake can lead to twenty more.\" [394] Officer Quinn smiled and turned around as Wheeler approached with a stack of papers. [395] \"Ah, Thomas is back,\" said Officer Quinn, looking cheerful, \"What did you find, Thomas?\" [396] \"Officer, uh,\" Willie quickly flipped through the stack of records, \"It's hard to say. This is half of the list, the other half hasn't been recorded yet. But I think you might find something interesting.\" [397] He handed Inspector Quinn a stack of papers hastily recording names and addresses. These were the names that Willie had recorded while questioning the audience at the officer's request. [398] The inspector and Ellery, who was standing beside him, began to carefully examine the list, carefully studying each name. Just as they were halfway through, Inspector Quinn suddenly tensed up. He squinted at the name that made him stop, then looked at Willie with some confusion. [399] \"Morgan?\" he said thoughtfully, \"Benjamin Morgan. It sounds familiar, Willie. Have you thought of anything?\" [400] Willie smiled faintly. \"I knew you'd ask me that, officer. Benjamin Morgan was the lawyer partner of Monte Field, and they split up two years ago.\" [401] Quin nodded. The three exchanged glances. Then, Quin shrugged and said succinctly, \"I think we should learn more about Mr. Morgan.\" [402] He sighed and continued to look at the list, studying each name intently. He occasionally looked up, lost in thought, shook his head, and then continued reading. Wiley knew that Officer Quin had an excellent memory, even better than Ellery, so he watched his superior with admiration. [403] Finally, Officer Quin handed the list back to Wiley. \"Nothing there, Thomas,\" he said, his tone serious. \"Did you notice anything I missed?\" [404] Wiley stared at the older man for a moment, said nothing, and simply shook his head before walking away. [405] \"Wait a minute, Thomas,\" Officer Quin called out. \"Before you finish recording the second half, have Mr. Morgan come to the manager's office, please. But don't scare him. By the way, make sure he has a ticket stub before he goes to the office.\" Quin spoke, and Wiley left. [406] As the police were busy completing the tasks assigned by the detectives, Poulzer stood by watching. So, Quinn gestured for him to come over. The short, plump manager hurried over. [407] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn asked, \"when do your janitors usually start cleaning?\" [408] \"Oh, they've been here for a while, waiting to start, Officer. Most theaters clean up in the morning, but I always have them come in after the evening performance. What's your plan?\" [409] Ellery frowned slightly at Quinn's question but brightened up when he heard Poulzer's response, starting to polish his glasses contentedly. [410] \"Mr. Poulzer,\" Quinn continued calmly, \"what I want you to do is, tonight, after everyone leaves, you arrange for the female janitors to thoroughly clean the place. They must pick up and put away everything \u2013 everything, no matter how insignificant it may seem. Tell them to pay special attention to ticket stubs. Are they reliable?\" [411] \"Oh, absolutely reliable, officer. They've been here since the theater was built. You can trust they won't miss any clues. What should I do with the items they clean out?\" [412] \"Carefully package them, write my name on them, and have a trustworthy person deliver them to headquarters tomorrow morning.\" Detective Quin paused, then added, \"Mr. Pell, I want to emphasize: this task is extremely important, far more so than it appears. Do you understand?\" [413] \"Of course, of course!\" Pell hurriedly left after that. [414] A balding detective strode briskly across the carpet, descending the left aisle, and tipped his hat to Detective Quin. He held a stack of records, similar to those brought by Detective Wiley. [415] \"Detective Wiley asked me to give these to you. They're the remaining audience list and addresses, officer.\" [416] Detective Quinn eagerly took the document from the agent. Ellery leaned forward. The old man slowly scanned the paper with his long fingers, moving from one name to another. As he neared the last page, he smiled, glancing at Ellery with excitement before finishing the page. He turned to his son and whispered a few words. Ellery listened intently, nodding in agreement. [417] Officer Quinn turned back to the waiting detective and said, \"Johnson, come here,\" as he unfolded the paper he had been studying and showed it to Johnson, \"You go find Wells and have him report to me immediately. After that, you go find this woman\u2014\" his finger pointed to a name, along with the corresponding row and seat number, \"and have her come with you to the manager's office. You'll find that a man named Morgan is already there. By the way, if they start talking, listen in\u2014I need to know what they're saying. Treat the lady with respect.\" [418] \"Understood, sir. Johnson also told me,\" Johnson continued, \"that he separated those without tickets and other spectators. He wants to know how to handle them.\" [419] \"Have their names appeared on both records, Johnson?\" Quin asked, handing him the second record to take back to Waley. [420] \"All of them, sir.\" [421] \"Then tell Wheeler to let them leave with the others, but before they go, he should record their names separately. I won't need to see them or talk to them again.\" [422] Johnson saluted and left. [423] Detective Quinn turned to Ellery and spoke quietly. Ellery had some ideas in his mind, but Pendergast's reappearance interrupted them. [424] \"Detective?\" Manager Pendergast coughed politely. [425] \"Oh, what is it, Pendergast?\" Detective Quinn quickly turned around and said, \"Is the cleaning lady doing well?\" [426] \"Yes, sir. Do you have any other orders? And, officer, I beg your pardon, I'd like to ask, how long will the audience have to wait? Many people have asked me this question, and I hope there won't be any trouble on this matter.\" [427] \"Oh, don't worry, Pensel,\" the officer said casually, \"they'll be leaving soon. In fact, I've already ordered my men to let them go in a few minutes. However, before they leave, there's something that will make them complain.\" [428] \"Really, sir?\" [429] \"Yes,\" Quinn said, \"they will be searched. They will undoubtedly protest, and you will hear their threats to sue you or even hit you. But don't worry. I'll take responsibility for everything that happened tonight, and I promise you won't have any trouble... Now, I need a female searcher to help us. We have a female officer here, but she's busy downstairs. You see, can you find a reliable woman for me? - preferably a middle-aged woman - who can accept this ungrateful job and keep it secret.\" [430] The manager thought for a moment. \"I think I can help you. Mrs. Philip, she is our costume custodian. She has been working here for many years and will definitely meet your expectations.\" [431] \"That's her,\" Quin said cheerfully, \"find her and send her to the main entrance. Inspector Willie will give her the necessary instructions.\" [432] Willie, who was walking over, heard the last sentence. Pensel hurried down the aisle, heading for the box. [433] \"Is Morgan ready?\" Quin asked. [434] \"Yes, sir.\" [435] \"Good, then you have one more thing to do, Thomas. I want you to supervise the audience in the main hall and the boxes, making sure they leave one by one. Thoroughly search them before they leave. Everyone must leave through the main entrance, and make sure the guards at other exits direct the audience to the back of the theater.\" [436] Williams nodded. \"Now, regarding the search - Piggott!\" [437] Erlie, who had been leaning lazily against a pillar, stood up and followed Piggott. Williams hurried over, and Quinn called out, \"Don't let the audience upstairs leave yet. Wait until the front row audience leaves before they can go. Send someone up to maintain order and keep them quiet.\" [438] After giving the last important instructions, Quinn turned to the guard nearby, Dole, and whispered, \"Go to the cloakroom, Dole. Keep a close eye on the coat when someone takes it. When they leave, thoroughly search the place. Bring anything left behind to me.\" [439] Erlie leaned against a pillar again, like a marble sentinel standing at the crime scene. He stood there, his eyes glazed over, his hands tightly gripping the collar of his coat. Flint, a broad-shouldered man, rushed over, his eyes shining with excitement. Detective Quinn looked at him cautiously. [440] \"Any discoveries, Flint?\" he asked, fumbling for his snuff box. [441] \"Very good, very good!\" Detective Quinn called out, \"Where did you find it?\" [442] \"Right inside the main entrance,\" Flint replied. \"It looks like the ticket holder dropped it as soon as they came in.\" [443] Detective Quinn didn't speak. His hand suddenly reached into his vest pocket and pulled out the blue ticket stub found on the deceased. He silently looked at them \u2013 two ticket stubs, the same color, one with \"LL32 Left\" printed on it, and the other with \"LL30 Left.\" [444] As he closely examined the innocent ticket stubs, his eyes narrowed into a line. He bent down to take a closer look, carefully aligning the bottoms of the ticket stubs; a hint of confusion appeared in his gray eyes, and then he aligned the tops of the ticket stubs. But no matter how he tried, the two ticket stubs did not match at the tear. [445] Nevertheless, the tearing positions of the two tickets were not consistent with each other. [446] Chapter 5: Interrogating Suspects\n[447] Officer Quinn walked across the wide red carpet laid in front of the theater's main entrance, lowering his hat to cover his eyes. He reached into his pocket, feeling for his indispensable snuff box. Officer Quinn seemed to be deep in thought, as he tightly held the two blue stubs, furrowing his brow in dissatisfaction. [448] He approached a door with green spots, marked \"Manager's Office.\" Before opening it, he turned around to survey the situation. The commotion among the audience had subsided significantly. Chatter filled the theater as police and investigators moved between the rows, issuing commands, answering questions, and ushering the audience out of their seats, lining them up in the main aisle to be searched at the main entrance. [449] Officer Quinn inadvertently noticed that very few people protested the tormenting ordeal. They seemed too exhausted to complain about the rude search. [450] The office's reception area was small, decorated with copper and oak. Parsons Johnny sat slumped in a chair against the wall, puffing away indifferently. A police officer stood next to the chair, a large hand resting on Parsons' shoulder. [451] \"Parsons, come with me,\" Inspector Quinn said, walking forward. The little scoundrel stood up lazily, deftly flicking his cigarette butt into a gleaming copper spittoon, trailing after the inspector with a lackluster air. The police officer followed close behind. [452] Inspector Quinn opened the door to the main office, stepping aside to let the scoundrel and the police officer go first. The door closed behind them with a bang. [453] Louis Posen had a unique taste in office decor. A carved table held a lamp with a transparent green shade. Chairs, ashtrays, a beautifully crafted coat rack, and a long sofa chair, all tastefully arranged in the room. Unlike most managers' offices, Posen did not hang photos of stars, agents, producers, or \"angels\" on the walls but rather hung several exquisite engravings, a large tapestry, and a painting by Constable. [454] However, at this moment, Inspector Quinn was not admiring Mr. Posen's personal office's artistic taste but rather observing the six people across from him. A middle-aged man sat next to Johnson, dressed in an impeccable evening suit, slightly overweight, and with very shrewd eyes, who was now frowning in puzzlement.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."